+ Compare first-semester #NormalTok("sleep_hours"); between students who persisted and those who subsequently left the study. Do dropouts exhibit sleep deficits alongside wellbeing deficits?
+ Compute the Pearson correlation between supervisor #NormalTok("support"); and academic #NormalTok("satisfaction");. Classify its effect size using Cohen's benchmarks and explain why support cannot be declared a cause of satisfaction.
+ For each demographic and study variable in #NormalTok("students");, select the appropriate descriptive summaries based on #ref(<tbl-summaries>, supplement: [Table]) and justify your choice in one sentence.

== Further reading
<further-reading-5>
- #emph[Python for Data Analysis] by Wes McKinney @mckinney2022: comprehensive coverage of data wrangling and descriptive aggregations with Pandas.
- #emph[Statistical Power Analysis for the Behavioral Sciences] @cohen1988: classic source for effect size benchmarks (small, medium, large correlations).

= Hypothesis Testing and Statistical Inference
<sec-ch07>
Research is almost always about more people than it can measure. A study of 600 graduate students is interesting because of what it says about graduate students in general, and a clinical trial of 200 patients matters because of the patients who will be treated after it. The step from the people measured to the people meant is called #strong[statistical inference]. It rests on one uncomfortable fact: a different sample would have given different numbers. Inference is the discipline of saying how different, and of deciding when a pattern in a sample is strong enough to be believed.

This chapter builds that discipline from the ground up. It starts with what happens when samples are drawn again and again, which leads to standard errors and confidence intervals. It then develops the logic of hypothesis testing by simulation, shuffling group labels until the meaning of a p-value can be seen, before turning to the formal tests that appear in almost every thesis. The hypotheses come from Chapter 5: whether graduate students sleep less than the recommended 7 hours (RQ2), and whether the wellbeing workshop improved wellbeing (RQ3).

