=== Type I and Type II errors
<type-i-and-type-ii-errors>
Because a test works with probabilities, its decision can be wrong in two ways, shown in #ref(<tbl-errors>, supplement: [Table]).

#figure([
#table(
  columns: 3,
  align: (auto,auto,auto,),
  table.header([], [$H_0$ is really true], [$H_0$ is really false],),
  table.hline(),
  [#strong[Reject $H_0$]], [Type I error (a false alarm)], [Correct],
  [#strong[Do not reject $H_0$]], [Correct], [Type II error (a missed effect)],
)
], caption: figure.caption(
position: top, 
[
The two kinds of error in hypothesis testing
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-tbl", 
supplement: "Table", 
)
<tbl-errors>


The significance level controls Type I errors: with $alpha = 0.05$, about 5% of tests of a true null hypothesis will be "significant" by chance alone. This can be demonstrated. The simulation below splits the students into two groups completely at random, so that no real difference can exist, tests whether their wellbeing differs, and repeats the whole procedure a thousand times:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("p_values ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("replicate");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1000");#NormalTok(", {");],
[#NormalTok("  random_group ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sample");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("rep");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"A\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"B\"");#NormalTok("), ");#DecValTok("300");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("t.test");#NormalTok("(first_sem");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("wellbeing ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" random_group)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("p.value");],
[#NormalTok("})");],
[#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(p_values ");#SpecialCharTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.05");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.065");],));
]
]
About 6% of the tests are "significant", even though every difference is pure chance, close to the 5% that the significance level predicts. A single significant result is therefore never the final word.

=== Statistical power
<statistical-power>
A Type II error, missing an effect that is really there, depends mainly on two things: the size of the effect and the size of the sample. A stricter significance level also makes effects harder to detect, which is one reason not to lower it without cause. The probability that a study detects an effect of a given size, when the effect is real, is its #strong[power] (Chapter 5). Power is easiest to understand by simulation. The function below imagines a study of the workshop in which the true effect is 5 points (with a standard deviation of 11, as in the real data), runs a t-test, and reports whether the result was significant. Running it 2,000 times for each group size shows how often studies of that size succeed:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("simulate_study ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#ControlFlowTok("function");#NormalTok("(n_per_group, ");#AttributeTok("effect =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("5");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("sd =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("11");#NormalTok(") {");],
