== Reporting the results
<reporting-the-results>
A test is reported with the statistic, its degrees of freedom (df, printed by R), the p-value, and, most importantly, the size of the effect with a confidence interval. Exact p-values are given to two or three decimal places, and very small ones as "#emph[p] \< .001". The report should also return to the hypothesis stated in advance and say whether the data contradicts the null hypothesis. The examples below follow these conventions.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
#strong[One-sample t-test.] Students slept less than the recommended 7 hours (#emph[M] = 6.48, 95% CI \[6.40, 6.57\]), #emph[t]\(593) = -12.18, #emph[p] \< .001.

#strong[Two-sample t-test.] Students invited to the wellbeing workshop reported higher wellbeing at the end of semester 2 (#emph[M] = 65.5) than those not invited (#emph[M] = 60.2), a difference of 5.2 points, 95% CI \[3.4, 7.1\], #emph[t]\(598) = 5.47, #emph[p] \< .001, #emph[d] = 0.45. A permutation test with 5,000 shuffles gave the same conclusion. The null hypothesis of no effect was therefore rejected.

#strong[Chi-square test.] Considering dropout was related to employment, $chi^2$\(2) = 10.89, #emph[p] = .004: 26% of students with full-time jobs had considered dropping out, compared with 14% of those without a job.

]
