<fig-partition>


If the groups really come from populations with the same average, the between-group variation should be no larger than the within-group variation would lead one to expect: group averages always differ a little by chance. ANOVA's #strong[F statistic] is the ratio of the two:

$ F = upright("variation between groups") / upright("variation within groups") $

An F close to 1 means that the group averages differ no more than chance would produce; a large F means that they differ more. The function #NormalTok("aov()"); fits the model, and #NormalTok("summary()"); shows the test:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("aov");#NormalTok("(stress ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" group, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" group_scores))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("            Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value  Pr(>F)   ");],
[#NormalTok("group        2  1.307  0.6533   10.69 0.00419 **");],
[#NormalTok("Residuals    9  0.550  0.0611                   ");],
[#NormalTok("---");],
[#NormalTok("Signif. codes:  0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1");],));
]
]
The p-value is small: these three groups differ.

The ratio matters more than the size of the differences between averages. The next code keeps the three group averages exactly as they are, but places the scores either close to their averages or far from them, and calculates F each time:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("offsets ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rep");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialCharTok("-");#FloatTok("0.3");#NormalTok(", ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#FloatTok("0.1");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("0.1");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("0.3");#NormalTok("), ");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("group_avg ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rep");#NormalTok("(group_means");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("mean, ");#AttributeTok("each =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("tight ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" group_scores ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("stress =");#NormalTok(" group_avg ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.5");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("*");#NormalTok(" offsets)");],
[#NormalTok("wide  ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" group_scores ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("stress =");#NormalTok(" group_avg ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("*");#NormalTok(" offsets)");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("tight =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("aov");#NormalTok("(stress ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" group, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" tight))[[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("]][[");#StringTok("\"F value\"");#NormalTok("]][");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("],");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("wide  =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("aov");#NormalTok("(stress ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" group, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" wide))[[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("]][[");#StringTok("\"F value\"");#NormalTok("]][");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("])");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("  tight    wide ");],
[#NormalTok("39.2000  0.6125 ");],));
]
]
The group averages are identical in both versions, yet F is large when the scores cluster tightly around their averages and small when they spread widely. Differences of about half a point between groups are convincing when students within a group hardly differ, and unremarkable when they differ by more than two points. This is the whole logic of ANOVA, and it is why group averages should never be compared without looking at the spread within groups (Chapter 4).

=== The student wellbeing data
<the-student-wellbeing-data>
The data comes first. #ref(<fig-stress-faculty>, supplement: [Figure]) shows stress in each faculty.

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(study, ");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" faculty, ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" stress)) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_boxplot");#NormalTok("() ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("labs");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("NULL");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Stress score (1 to 5)\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("theme_minimal");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("base_size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("12");#NormalTok(")");],));
#figure([
#box(image("08-anova-regression_files/figure-typst/fig-stress-faculty-1.svg", alt: "Five box plots of stress scores, one per faculty. Health Sciences is highest and Humanities lowest, with much overlap between all faculties."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Stress scores by faculty.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-stress-faculty>


The faculties overlap a lot, but Health Sciences sits a little higher and Humanities a little lower. The ANOVA tests whether these differences exceed what chance would produce:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("stress_anova ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("aov");#NormalTok("(stress ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" faculty, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" study)");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(stress_anova)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("             Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value  Pr(>F)   ");],
[#NormalTok("faculty       4   8.72  2.1788   4.152 0.00252 **");],
[#NormalTok("Residuals   595 312.19  0.5247                   ");],
[#NormalTok("---");],
[#NormalTok("Signif. codes:  0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1");],));
]
]
The p-value is 0.003, so the faculties do differ in stress. The size of the difference is measured by #strong[eta squared] ($eta^2$), the share of all the variation in stress that lies between faculties:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("ss ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(stress_anova)[[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("]][[");#StringTok("\"Sum Sq\"");#NormalTok("]]");],
[#NormalTok("ss[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("] ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sum");#NormalTok("(ss)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.02715776");],));
]
]
Only about 3% of the differences in stress between students have to do with their faculty; the rest is about the individual students. By common guidelines, an $eta^2$ of 0.01 is small, 0.06 medium, and 0.14 large @cohen1988, so this is a real but small effect. For the research question, the answer is that faculty matters, but knowing a student's faculty says very little about how stressed that student is.

=== Post-hoc tests
<post-hoc-tests>
ANOVA says that the faculties differ, but not which ones. #strong[Post-hoc tests] compare every pair while keeping the overall chance of a false alarm at 5%. Tukey's test is the most common:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("TukeyHSD");#NormalTok("(stress_anova)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("  Tukey multiple comparisons of means");],
[#NormalTok("    95% family-wise confidence level");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Fit: aov(formula = stress ~ faculty, data = study)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("$faculty");],
[#NormalTok("                                        diff          lwr         upr     p adj");],
[#NormalTok("Health Sciences-Education         0.19429917 -0.033842346  0.42244068 0.1367660");],
[#NormalTok("Humanities-Education             -0.14304647 -0.403603347  0.11751041 0.5614602");],
[#NormalTok("Natural Sciences-Education       -0.05877343 -0.326527149  0.20898029 0.9749738");],
[#NormalTok("Social Sciences-Education         0.12216527 -0.123607736  0.36793827 0.6535324");],
[#NormalTok("Humanities-Health Sciences       -0.33734564 -0.595910542 -0.07878073 0.0035331");],
[#NormalTok("Natural Sciences-Health Sciences -0.25307260 -0.518888282  0.01274309 0.0707118");],
[#NormalTok("Social Sciences-Health Sciences  -0.07213390 -0.315794100  0.17152630 0.9275445");],
[#NormalTok("Natural Sciences-Humanities       0.08427304 -0.209834619  0.37838070 0.9352210");],
[#NormalTok("Social Sciences-Humanities        0.26521174 -0.009035651  0.53945912 0.0635851");],
[#NormalTok("Social Sciences-Natural Sciences  0.18093870 -0.100155231  0.46203263 0.3973784");],));
]
]
Each row compares two faculties: the difference in average stress, its confidence interval, and an adjusted p-value (#NormalTok("p adj");). Only one comparison is significant: Health Sciences students are more stressed than Humanities students, by about 0.34 points on the 1-to-5 scale. The other differences are small enough to be chance.

=== Assumptions of ANOVA
<assumptions-of-anova>
ANOVA makes the same assumptions as the t-test: independent observations, roughly normal data within each group (or large groups), and similar spread in each group. The spread can be checked with #strong[Levene's test], from the #strong[car] package:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(car)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Loading required package: carData");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("Attaching package: 'car'");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("The following object is masked from 'package:dplyr':");],
[],
[#NormalTok("    recode");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("leveneTest");#NormalTok("(stress ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("factor");#NormalTok("(faculty), ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" study)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Levene's Test for Homogeneity of Variance (center = median)");],
[#NormalTok("       Df F value Pr(>F)");],
[#NormalTok("group   4  0.9429 0.4386");],
[#NormalTok("      595               ");],));
]
]
The p-value is large, so there is no evidence that the spread differs between faculties. When the assumptions fail, the #strong[Kruskal-Wallis test] is the non-parametric alternative, comparing ranks instead of averages:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("kruskal.test");#NormalTok("(stress ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" faculty, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" study)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Kruskal-Wallis rank sum test");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  stress by faculty");],
[#NormalTok("Kruskal-Wallis chi-squared = 15.46, df = 4, p-value = 0.003836");],));
]
]
It agrees with the ANOVA.

== Two-way ANOVA
<two-way-anova>
Wellbeing might depend on the programme (Master's or PhD), on study mode (full-time or part-time), or on a combination of both. A #strong[two-way ANOVA] examines all three possibilities at once. The #strong[main effect] of programme is the difference between Master's and PhD students on average, and the main effect of study mode is the difference between full-time and part-time students. The #strong[interaction] asks whether the effect of study mode #emph[depends on] the programme: part-time study might, for example, be harder on PhD students than on Master's students.

An #strong[interaction plot] shows the group averages as lines. If the lines are parallel, there is no interaction: the effect of study mode is the same in both programmes.

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("study ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("summarise");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("wellbeing =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(wellbeing), ");#AttributeTok(".by =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(programme, study_mode)) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" programme, ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" wellbeing, ");#AttributeTok("colour =");#NormalTok(" study_mode, ");#AttributeTok("group =");#NormalTok(" study_mode)) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_line");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("linewidth =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_point");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("scale_colour_viridis_d");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("end =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.8");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("labs");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("NULL");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Average wellbeing\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("colour =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Study mode\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("theme_minimal");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("base_size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("13");#NormalTok(")");],));
#figure([
#box(image("08-anova-regression_files/figure-typst/fig-interaction-1.svg", alt: "Two lines, for full-time and part-time students, across Master's and PhD. Part-time is lower in both programmes; the lines are roughly parallel."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Average first-semester wellbeing by programme and study mode.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-interaction>


Part-time students have lower wellbeing in both programmes, and the lines are close to parallel. In the formula, #NormalTok("programme * study_mode"); asks for both main effects and their interaction:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("aov");#NormalTok("(wellbeing ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" programme ");#SpecialCharTok("*");#NormalTok(" study_mode, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" study))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                      Df Sum Sq Mean Sq F value   Pr(>F)    ");],
[#NormalTok("programme              1    316   315.6   2.230 0.135913    ");],
[#NormalTok("study_mode             1   1640  1640.2  11.587 0.000709 ***");],
[#NormalTok("programme:study_mode   1    119   118.6   0.838 0.360341    ");],
[#NormalTok("Residuals            596  84364   141.6                     ");],
[#NormalTok("---");],
[#NormalTok("Signif. codes:  0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1");],));
]
]
Study mode has a clear effect. Programme does not, and neither does the interaction: part-time study lowers wellbeing by about the same amount whether a student is doing a Master's or a PhD. When an interaction #emph[is] significant, it should be interpreted before the main effects, because it means that no single "effect of study mode" describes everyone.

== Linear regression
<linear-regression>
ANOVA compares groups. #strong[Regression] goes further: it models how an outcome changes with one or more #strong[predictors], which can be numeric or categorical. It is the workhorse of quantitative research, and ANOVA is in fact a special case of it.

=== The regression line
<the-regression-line>
At its heart, regression describes the #strong[average outcome for given values of the predictors]. For sleep and GPA, the question is what the average GPA is among students who sleep 5 hours, among those who sleep 6 hours, and so on. These averages can be calculated directly, by grouping students into half-hour bands of sleep:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("sleep_bands ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" study ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialCharTok("!");#FunctionTok("is.na");#NormalTok("(sleep_hours)) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("sleep_band =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(sleep_hours ");#SpecialCharTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("summarise");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("mean_gpa =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(gpa), ");#AttributeTok("students =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("n");#NormalTok("(), ");#AttributeTok(".by =");#NormalTok(" sleep_band) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("arrange");#NormalTok("(sleep_band)");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_bands");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("   sleep_band mean_gpa students");],
[#NormalTok("1         3.5 2.882000        5");],
[#NormalTok("2         4.0 2.901250        8");],
[#NormalTok("3         4.5 2.939231       13");],
[#NormalTok("4         5.0 3.022340       47");],
[#NormalTok("5         5.5 3.021385       65");],
[#NormalTok("6         6.0 3.054951      103");],
[#NormalTok("7         6.5 3.094519      104");],
[#NormalTok("8         7.0 3.120000      102");],
[#NormalTok("9         7.5 3.175957       94");],
[#NormalTok("10        8.0 3.320606       33");],
[#NormalTok("11        8.5 3.206429       14");],
[#NormalTok("12        9.0 3.720000        2");],
[#NormalTok("13        9.5 3.160000        1");],
[#NormalTok("14       10.0 3.146667        3");],));
]
]
#ref(<fig-conditional-means>, supplement: [Figure]) places these averages over the individual students, together with a straight regression line.

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(study, ");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" sleep_hours, ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" gpa)) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_point");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("alpha =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.2");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("colour =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"grey40\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_smooth");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("method =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"lm\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("se =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("FALSE");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_point");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" sleep_bands, ");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" sleep_band, ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" mean_gpa, ");#AttributeTok("size =");#NormalTok(" students),");],
[#NormalTok("             ");#AttributeTok("colour =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"#b2182b\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("labs");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Hours of sleep per night\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"GPA\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("size =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Students\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("theme_minimal");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("base_size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("12");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("`geom_smooth()` using formula = 'y ~ x'");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning: Removed 6 rows containing non-finite outside the scale range");],
[#NormalTok("(`stat_smooth()`).");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning: Removed 6 rows containing missing values or values outside the scale range");],
[#NormalTok("(`geom_point()`).");],));
]
#figure([
#box(image("08-anova-regression_files/figure-typst/fig-conditional-means-1.svg", alt: "Scatter plot of GPA against sleep with grey points, red points for band averages that rise gently from left to right, and a straight blue line passing close to the red points."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
GPA and sleep. Grey points are students; red points are the average GPA in each half-hour band of sleep, sized by the number of students; the line is the linear regression. The line is a smooth summary of the red averages.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-conditional-means>


The band averages rise gently with sleep, and the line passes close to them, especially where there are many students. The regression line is a smooth summary of these conditional averages: it assumes that the average GPA changes by the same amount for each extra hour of sleep, and estimates that amount from all the students at once. The spread of the grey points around the line is what the model does not explain.

A small example shows the calculation. Here are five students' sleep and GPA:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("five ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("tibble");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("sleep =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("5");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("6.5");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("8");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("gpa   =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("2.8");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.2");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.1");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.4");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok(")");],));
]
#strong[Simple linear regression] fits the straight line that best describes how GPA changes with sleep:

$ upright("GPA") = b_0 + b_1 times upright("sleep") + upright("error") $

The coefficient $b_0$ is the #strong[intercept], the predicted GPA for a student who sleeps zero hours, and $b_1$ is the #strong[slope], the change in predicted GPA for each extra hour of sleep. "Best" means the line that makes the squared vertical distances between the points and the line, the #strong[residuals], as small as possible, a method called #strong[least squares]. The function #NormalTok("lm()");, for #emph[linear model], finds it:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("lm");#NormalTok("(gpa ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" sleep, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" five)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("Call:");],
[#NormalTok("lm(formula = gpa ~ sleep, data = five)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Coefficients:");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept)        sleep  ");],
[#NormalTok("      1.865        0.190  ");],));
]
]
Each extra hour of sleep goes with a GPA that is about 0.19 higher. The intercept is where the line would cross zero hours of sleep, which no one does; it is needed to place the line, but it rarely means anything on its own.

=== The student wellbeing data
<the-student-wellbeing-data-1>
For all 600 students, #NormalTok("summary()"); gives the full results:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("gpa_simple ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lm");#NormalTok("(gpa ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" sleep_hours, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" study)");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(gpa_simple)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("Call:");],
[#NormalTok("lm(formula = gpa ~ sleep_hours, data = study)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Residuals:");],
[#NormalTok("     Min       1Q   Median       3Q      Max ");],
[#NormalTok("-0.97283 -0.20475 -0.00188  0.21898  0.94606 ");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Coefficients:");],
[#NormalTok("            Estimate Std. Error t value Pr(>|t|)    ");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept)  2.57713    0.08316   30.99  < 2e-16 ***");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_hours  0.08081    0.01267    6.38 3.57e-10 ***");],
[#NormalTok("---");],
[#NormalTok("Signif. codes:  0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Residual standard error: 0.3189 on 592 degrees of freedom");],
[#NormalTok("  (6 observations deleted due to missingness)");],
[#NormalTok("Multiple R-squared:  0.06433,   Adjusted R-squared:  0.06275 ");],
[#NormalTok("F-statistic:  40.7 on 1 and 592 DF,  p-value: 3.573e-10");],));
]
]
The output contains three results that matter for the research question. The #strong[coefficients] table gives the estimate of each coefficient, its standard error, a t statistic, and a p-value testing whether it is zero. The slope for #NormalTok("sleep_hours"); is about 0.08: each extra hour of sleep goes with a GPA about 0.08 higher, and the p-value shows that this is not chance. #strong[R-squared] is the share of the variation in GPA that the model explains; here it is only 0.06, so sleep explains about 6% of the differences in GPA. Sleep matters, but it is far from the whole story, as the scatter plot suggested. Finally, the #strong[residual standard error] is the typical distance between a student's actual GPA and the line, about 0.32 grade points.

=== Multiple regression
<multiple-regression>
Many things affect grades at once. #strong[Multiple regression] includes several predictors in one model:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("gpa_model ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lm");#NormalTok("(gpa ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" sleep_hours ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" study_hours ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" stress ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" support, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" study)");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(gpa_model)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("Call:");],
[#NormalTok("lm(formula = gpa ~ sleep_hours + study_hours + stress + support, ");],
[#NormalTok("    data = study)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Residuals:");],
[#NormalTok("     Min       1Q   Median       3Q      Max ");],
[#NormalTok("-0.86953 -0.19437  0.00526  0.18957  0.76429 ");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Coefficients:");],
[#NormalTok("             Estimate Std. Error t value Pr(>|t|)    ");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept)  2.137403   0.145279  14.712  < 2e-16 ***");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_hours  0.106041   0.014163   7.487 2.62e-13 ***");],
[#NormalTok("study_hours  0.006928   0.001105   6.270 7.06e-10 ***");],
[#NormalTok("stress      -0.083526   0.017827  -4.685 3.48e-06 ***");],
[#NormalTok("support      0.110733   0.016696   6.632 7.57e-11 ***");],
[#NormalTok("---");],
[#NormalTok("Signif. codes:  0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Residual standard error: 0.2857 on 582 degrees of freedom");],
[#NormalTok("  (13 observations deleted due to missingness)");],
[#NormalTok("Multiple R-squared:  0.2525,    Adjusted R-squared:  0.2473 ");],
[#NormalTok("F-statistic: 49.14 on 4 and 582 DF,  p-value: < 2.2e-16");],));
]
]
Each coefficient now means the change in predicted GPA for a one-unit increase in that predictor, #strong[holding the other predictors constant]. Among students with the same study hours, stress, and support, each extra hour of sleep goes with a GPA about 0.11 higher. Each extra hour of study per week adds about 0.007, each point of stress lowers GPA by about 0.08, and each point of supervisor support raises it by about 0.11. All four are significant.

Together, the four predictors explain about 25% of the variation in GPA. That may sound modest, but grades depend on many things no survey measures, from ability to luck on exam day. In social and educational research, models that explain a quarter of the variation are common and useful. The #strong[adjusted R-squared] is slightly lower, because it corrects for the number of predictors: adding any predictor, even a useless one, raises R-squared a little, but only a useful one raises the adjusted version.

The output also contains the line #NormalTok("(13 observations deleted due to missingness)");: #NormalTok("lm()"); leaves out students with a missing value in any variable of the model. The number of students each model is based on should always be reported.

=== Categorical predictors
<categorical-predictors>
Categorical variables can be predictors too. Gender, for example, can be added to the model to see whether it matters once the other predictors are taken into account:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("gpa_gender ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lm");#NormalTok("(gpa ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" sleep_hours ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" study_hours ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" stress ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" support ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" gender, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" study)");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(gpa_gender)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("coefficients");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                Estimate  Std. Error    t value     Pr(>|t|)");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept)  2.123737610 0.147260520 14.4216360 1.592391e-40");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_hours  0.106600187 0.014203710  7.5050947 2.318148e-13");],
[#NormalTok("study_hours  0.006995075 0.001111694  6.2922647 6.163042e-10");],
[#NormalTok("stress      -0.082830266 0.017877519 -4.6332081 4.447816e-06");],
[#NormalTok("support      0.110525008 0.016709773  6.6143931 8.474218e-11");],
[#NormalTok("genderMale   0.013815196 0.023831664  0.5796992 5.623422e-01");],));
]
]
R turns a categorical predictor into a comparison with a #strong[reference category], by default the first in alphabetical order: here #NormalTok("Female");. The coefficient #NormalTok("genderMale"); is the difference between men and women, holding everything else constant. It is tiny and far from significant: there is no evidence that gender affects GPA. That is a finding too, and worth reporting.

For a variable with more categories, such as faculty, R compares each category with the reference, giving one coefficient for each of the others. A different reference is chosen with #NormalTok("relevel(factor(faculty), ref = \"Humanities\")");.

=== Separating an effect from a confounder
<separating-an-effect-from-a-confounder>
Chapters 5 and 6 found that students who take more caffeine have lower GPAs, but also sleep less. Two explanations are possible, and #ref(<fig-confounder>, supplement: [Figure]) draws them. In the first, caffeine itself harms grades. In the second, sleep is a common cause: students who sleep less both drink more coffee and get lower grades, and caffeine has no effect of its own.

#figure([
#block[
#box(image("08-anova-regression_files\\figure-typst\\mermaid-figure-1.png", height: 7.66in, width: 2.7in))

]
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Two explanations for the link between caffeine and GPA. Left: caffeine affects GPA. Right: sleep affects both caffeine and GPA, which creates a link between them even if caffeine has no effect.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-confounder>


Regression can tell the two apart, by comparing students who sleep the same amount. Caffeine alone comes first:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("lm");#NormalTok("(gpa ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" caffeine_mg, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" study))");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("coefficients");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                 Estimate   Std. Error    t value     Pr(>|t|)");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept)  3.1849088129 2.195535e-02 145.063026 0.000000e+00");],
[#NormalTok("caffeine_mg -0.0004331688 9.239351e-05  -4.688303 3.418851e-06");],));
]
]
Caffeine has a significant negative association with GPA. Sleep is then added to the model:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("lm");#NormalTok("(gpa ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" caffeine_mg ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" sleep_hours, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" study))");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("coefficients");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                 Estimate   Std. Error    t value     Pr(>|t|)");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept)  2.6380932284 0.1238298172 21.3041841 4.534485e-75");],
[#NormalTok("caffeine_mg -0.0000780726 0.0001194238 -0.6537443 5.135321e-01");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_hours  0.0738597698 0.0165663746  4.4584148 9.891313e-06");],));
]
]
Once sleep is held constant, caffeine's coefficient shrinks to almost nothing and is no longer significant, while sleep stays significant. Among students who sleep the same amount, caffeine makes no difference to grades. Caffeine looked harmful only because heavy caffeine users sleep less: sleep was a #strong[confounder], and the data supports the second explanation.

The general rule is that a confounder is a common cause of the predictor and the outcome. Adjusting for it in a regression compares like with like, students who are the same on the confounder, and removes the misleading part of the association.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
Regression can remove the effect of a confounder only if you measured it and put it in the model. It cannot adjust for anything you did not measure. That is why, outside a randomised experiment like the workshop, regression shows associations #emph[adjusted for] the variables in the model, not proof of cause.

]
, 
title: 
[
Regression adjusts only for what you include
]
, 
background_color: 
rgb("#f7dddc")
, 
icon_color: 
rgb("#CC1914")
, 
icon: 
fa-exclamation()
, 
body_background_color: 
white
)
]
=== Curved relationships
<curved-relationships>
A straight line assumes that every extra hour of study adds the same amount to GPA, which is hard to believe: the difference between 5 and 15 hours a week is probably larger than the difference between 45 and 55. Adding a squared term lets the line curve:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("gpa_curve ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lm");#NormalTok("(gpa ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" study_hours ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("I");#NormalTok("(study_hours");#SpecialCharTok("^");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("), ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" study)");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(gpa_curve)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("coefficients");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                      Estimate   Std. Error   t value      Pr(>|t|)");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept)       2.8630064457 5.380474e-02 53.211044 3.718109e-227");],
[#NormalTok("study_hours       0.0183480169 3.914705e-03  4.686947  3.448155e-06");],
[#NormalTok("I(study_hours^2) -0.0002874497 6.488183e-05 -4.430358  1.121887e-05");],));
]
]
In the formula, #NormalTok("I()"); tells R to calculate #NormalTok("study_hours^2"); before fitting. The squared term is negative and significant: the line bends downwards. #ref(<fig-curve>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the shape.

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(study, ");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" study_hours, ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" gpa)) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_point");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("alpha =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.3");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_smooth");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("method =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"lm\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("formula =");#NormalTok(" y ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" x ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("I");#NormalTok("(x");#SpecialCharTok("^");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("labs");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Study hours per week\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"GPA\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("theme_minimal");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("base_size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("13");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning: Removed 8 rows containing non-finite outside the scale range");],
[#NormalTok("(`stat_smooth()`).");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning: Removed 8 rows containing missing values or values outside the scale range");],
[#NormalTok("(`geom_point()`).");],));
]
#figure([
#box(image("08-anova-regression_files/figure-typst/fig-curve-1.svg", alt: "Scatter plot of GPA against study hours, with a curve that rises steeply at first and then flattens out."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
GPA and weekly study hours, with a curved (quadratic) trend line.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-curve>


The first hours of study make a real difference; the curve then flattens, and reaches its highest point at about 32 hours a week, beyond which more study brings nothing extra. Economists call this #strong[diminishing returns], and it is worth knowing for any student tempted to study all night instead of sleeping.

=== Interactions
<interactions>
The effect of supervisor support need not be the same for every student. An #strong[interaction] term lets the effect of one predictor depend on another. In the formula, #NormalTok("support * programme"); gives the effect of support, the effect of programme, and how the effect of support differs between programmes. To make the coefficients easier to read, support is first #strong[centred]: the average is subtracted, so that zero means "average support":

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("study ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" study ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("support_c =");#NormalTok(" support ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(support))");],
[#NormalTok("gpa_interaction ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lm");#NormalTok("(gpa ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" support_c ");#SpecialCharTok("*");#NormalTok(" programme ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" sleep_hours ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" study_hours, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" study)");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(gpa_interaction)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("coefficients");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                         Estimate  Std. Error    t value     Pr(>|t|)");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept)            2.15459277 0.112180214 19.2065312 5.044803e-64");],
[#NormalTok("support_c              0.10210747 0.018389506  5.5524856 4.288901e-08");],
[#NormalTok("programmePhD           0.01173221 0.026136331  0.4488851 6.536819e-01");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_hours            0.11771209 0.014038094  8.3851901 3.842792e-16");],
[#NormalTok("study_hours            0.00656252 0.001109605  5.9142870 5.691946e-09");],
[#NormalTok("support_c:programmePhD 0.13416356 0.035844599  3.7429227 1.999717e-04");],));
]
]
For Master's students (the reference category), each point of support goes with a GPA about 0.10 higher. The interaction coefficient, #NormalTok("support_c:programmePhD");, says that for PhD students the effect is larger, by about 0.13, making it about 0.24. Supervisor support matters about 2.3 times as much for PhD students, which makes sense: a PhD depends more heavily on the supervisor.

=== Model diagnostics
<model-diagnostics>
Linear regression assumes that the relationship is linear (or modelled as curved, as above), that the residuals have similar spread everywhere, and that they are roughly normal. Violations show up as patterns in a plot of the residuals against the fitted values. It helps to know what those patterns look like before judging a real model, and simulated data can show them. The code below creates three datasets: one that meets the assumptions, one with a curved relationship fitted by a straight line, and one whose spread grows with the predictor. It fits a straight line to each and plots the residuals:

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("x ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("runif");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("200");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("simulated ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("list");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#StringTok("\"Assumptions met\"");#NormalTok("  ");#OtherTok("=");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.5");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("*");#NormalTok(" x ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rnorm");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("200");#NormalTok("),");],
