All three methods point to four dimensions, matching the four scales the questionnaire was designed to measure.

=== PCA and factor analysis compared
<pca-and-factor-analysis-compared>
PCA and factor analysis are often confused, and many theses use one when they mean the other. The difference lies in what they assume. #strong[PCA] simply summarises the variables: the components are combinations of the items, with no claim about why the items are related, and it is used to reduce many variables to a few, for example before a further analysis. #strong[Factor analysis] assumes the model of the first section: that each item is #emph[caused] by one or more latent variables, called #strong[factors], plus error of its own. A student answers the stress items the way they do #emph[because] they are stressed. Factor analysis is therefore the method for checking whether a questionnaire measures the constructs it is meant to, and that is the study's question.

== Factor analysis
<factor-analysis>
#strong[Exploratory factor analysis] (EFA) estimates how strongly each item is related to each factor. These relationships are called #strong[loadings]. Loadings run roughly from −1 to 1: close to 0 means that the item is unrelated to the factor, and a value above about 0.4 in size is usually considered a clear relationship.

The function #NormalTok("fa()"); from the psych package runs the analysis. Its main arguments are the number of factors (four, from parallel analysis), the #strong[rotation], and the estimation method. Rotation turns the factors to make them easier to interpret; "oblimin" allows the factors to correlate with each other, which is realistic, since stress and burnout surely go together. The option #NormalTok("fm = \"ml\""); uses maximum likelihood, a common estimation method:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("efa ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("fa");#NormalTok("(items, ");#AttributeTok("nfactors =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("rotate =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"oblimin\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("fm =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"ml\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Loading required namespace: GPArotation");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("print");#NormalTok("(efa");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("loadings, ");#AttributeTok("cutoff =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.3");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("sort =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("Loadings:");],
[#NormalTok("               ML1    ML3    ML2    ML4   ");],
[#NormalTok("support_1       0.749                     ");],
[#NormalTok("support_2       0.718                     ");],
[#NormalTok("support_3       0.669                     ");],
[#NormalTok("support_4       0.638                     ");],
[#NormalTok("support_5       0.726                     ");],
[#NormalTok("support_6       0.619                     ");],
[#NormalTok("stress_1               0.623              ");],
[#NormalTok("stress_3               0.627              ");],
[#NormalTok("stress_4              -0.503              ");],
[#NormalTok("stress_5               0.827              ");],
[#NormalTok("stress_6               0.547              ");],
[#NormalTok("burnout_1                     0.647       ");],
[#NormalTok("burnout_2                     0.719       ");],
[#NormalTok("burnout_4                     0.622       ");],
[#NormalTok("burnout_5                     0.544       ");],
[#NormalTok("burnout_6                     0.675       ");],
[#NormalTok("satisfaction_1                       0.659");],
[#NormalTok("satisfaction_2                       0.684");],
[#NormalTok("satisfaction_3                       0.585");],
[#NormalTok("satisfaction_4                       0.673");],
[#NormalTok("stress_2               0.496              ");],
[#NormalTok("burnout_3              0.325  0.446       ");],
[],
[#NormalTok("                ML1  ML3   ML2   ML4");],
[#NormalTok("SS loadings    2.87 2.41 2.384 1.735");],
[#NormalTok("Proportion Var 0.13 0.11 0.108 0.079");],
[#NormalTok("Cumulative Var 0.13 0.24 0.348 0.427");],));
]
]
In the printout, #NormalTok("cutoff = 0.3"); hides small loadings so that the pattern is easy to see, and #NormalTok("sort = TRUE"); groups the items by the factor they load on most. Unlike PCA, #NormalTok("fa()"); uses every student, including those who skipped an item.

The table shows four clear factors. Each collects the items of one scale: the six support items on one, the stress items on another, the burnout items on a third, and the four satisfaction items on the fourth. The names ML1 to ML4 are only labels; naming the factors is the researcher's job. The reversed item, #NormalTok("stress_4"); ("I feel confident handling problems in my studies"), loads #emph[negatively] on the stress factor, which is exactly right for a reversed item: agreeing with it means less stress, and it is why the item must be reversed before the stress score is calculated (Chapter 3). One item, #NormalTok("burnout_3"); ("Deadlines make me feel overwhelmed"), loads on both the burnout and the stress factor. Reading the item, that makes sense: feeling overwhelmed by deadlines is both. Such a #strong[cross-loading] item is worth discussing in a thesis, and sometimes worth rewording or removing in future studies.

Because the rotation allowed the factors to correlate, #NormalTok("fa()"); also estimates how strongly they do:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(efa");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("Phi, ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("      ML1   ML3   ML2   ML4");],
[#NormalTok("ML1  1.00 -0.37 -0.23  0.48");],
[#NormalTok("ML3 -0.37  1.00  0.66 -0.44");],
[#NormalTok("ML2 -0.23  0.66  1.00 -0.39");],
[#NormalTok("ML4  0.48 -0.44 -0.39  1.00");],));
]
]
The stress and burnout factors correlate strongly (about 0.66), but not so strongly that they are the same thing. The support and satisfaction factors correlate positively with each other and negatively with stress and burnout. These relationships make sense, which is itself evidence that the scales measure what they should.

=== Reliability and Cronbach's alpha
<reliability-and-cronbachs-alpha>
Factor analysis shows that the items of each scale belong together. #strong[Reliability] asks a related question: whether the items of a scale give consistent results (Chapter 5). The most common measure of this internal consistency is #strong[Cronbach's alpha], which ranges from 0 to 1. It rises when the items correlate strongly with each other, and also when there are more items, for the reason shown by the simulation at the start of the chapter. As a rough guide, 0.7 or above is acceptable and 0.8 or above good for research use. The function #NormalTok("alpha()"); from the psych package calculates it, after reversed items have been reversed:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("q ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" questionnaire");],
[#NormalTok("q");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("stress_4 ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" q");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("stress_4");],
[],
[#NormalTok("psych");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("alpha");#NormalTok("(q[, ");#FunctionTok("paste0");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"stress_\"");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(")])");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("total");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("raw_alpha");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.826084");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("psych");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("alpha");#NormalTok("(q[, ");#FunctionTok("paste0");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"burnout_\"");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(")])");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("total");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("raw_alpha");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.8313142");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("psych");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("alpha");#NormalTok("(q[, ");#FunctionTok("paste0");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"support_\"");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(")])");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("total");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("raw_alpha");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.8454391");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("psych");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("alpha");#NormalTok("(q[, ");#FunctionTok("paste0");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"satisfaction_\"");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(")])");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("total");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("raw_alpha");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.7642397");],));
]
]
All four scales are reliable, with alphas between 0.76 and 0.85. The full output of #NormalTok("alpha()"); also shows what alpha would be if each item were dropped, which helps to spot a weak item.

The code writes #NormalTok("psych::alpha()"); rather than #NormalTok("alpha()"); because ggplot2 also has a function called #NormalTok("alpha()");, for making colours transparent. Whichever package is loaded last wins, so after loading ggplot2 (or a package that loads it, such as factoextra) a plain #NormalTok("alpha()"); no longer calculates Cronbach's alpha. The #NormalTok("package::function()"); form always calls the intended function, whether or not the package is loaded.

A high alpha shows consistency, not validity. A scale can be highly reliable and still measure the wrong thing, as the target picture in Chapter 5 showed; the factor structure and the relationships with other constructs are the evidence for validity.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
A typical report: "An exploratory factor analysis (maximum likelihood, oblimin rotation) supported four factors, as indicated by parallel analysis. All items loaded on their intended factor (loadings 0.45 to 0.83 in absolute value), with one item (#emph[burnout\_3]) also loading on the stress factor. Internal consistency was acceptable to good (Cronbach's α = 0.76 to 0.85)."

A further step, #strong[confirmatory factor analysis], tests whether a structure decided in advance fits the data, rather than exploring it. It is done with the #strong[lavaan] package and is beyond this book.

