== Cluster analysis
<cluster-analysis>
Factor analysis groups #emph[variables]. #strong[Cluster analysis] groups #emph[people]: it looks for groups of students whose profiles are similar to each other and different from other groups. It is a descriptive method. It does not test a hypothesis, and the groups it finds are summaries of the data, useful for describing and thinking about students, not discoveries of natural kinds of student. The study's question about student profiles (RQ7) is accordingly exploratory (Chapter 5).

Each student is described by their average sleep, study hours, caffeine, and exercise across the semesters, and their stress, support, and satisfaction scores:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("scores ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" q ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("stress       =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rowMeans");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("pick");#NormalTok("(stress_1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#NormalTok("stress_6), ");#AttributeTok("na.rm =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("support      =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rowMeans");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("pick");#NormalTok("(support_1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#NormalTok("support_6), ");#AttributeTok("na.rm =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("satisfaction =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rowMeans");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("pick");#NormalTok("(satisfaction_1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#NormalTok("satisfaction_4), ");#AttributeTok("na.rm =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("  ) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("select");#NormalTok("(student_id, stress, support, satisfaction)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("profiles ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" semesters ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("summarise");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("across");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(sleep_hours, study_hours, caffeine_mg, exercise_days),");],
[#NormalTok("                   ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(.x, ");#AttributeTok("na.rm =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")),");],
[#NormalTok("            ");#AttributeTok(".by =");#NormalTok(" student_id) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("left_join");#NormalTok("(scores, ");#FunctionTok("join_by");#NormalTok("(student_id)) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("na.omit");#NormalTok("()");],
[],
[#NormalTok("profile_data ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("scale");#NormalTok("(profiles ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("select");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok("student_id))");],));
]
Clustering works with #strong[distances] between students: how different two students' profiles are. Caffeine is measured in hundreds of milligrams and sleep in single hours, so without adjustment caffeine would dominate every distance. The function #NormalTok("scale()"); turns every variable into z-scores (Chapter 6), so each counts equally.

=== k-means clustering
<k-means-clustering>
#strong[k-means] is the most widely used clustering method. The researcher chooses the number of clusters, $k$, and the algorithm places $k$ starting points at random, assigns each student to the nearest point, moves each point to the centre (the mean) of its students, and repeats the last two steps until nothing changes. Because the result depends on the random start, #NormalTok("nstart = 25"); runs the algorithm from 25 different starts and keeps the best result.

=== The number of clusters
<the-number-of-clusters>
As with components, no single rule decides the number of clusters, and two guides are common. The #strong[elbow method] uses the fact that the total distance of students from their cluster centres always falls as $k$ grows, and looks for the point where adding clusters stops helping much. The #strong[silhouette] measures, for each student, how much closer they are to their own cluster than to the next nearest one, from −1 to 1; a higher average silhouette means clearer clusters.

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("123");#NormalTok(")");],
[#FunctionTok("fviz_nbclust");#NormalTok("(profile_data, kmeans, ");#AttributeTok("method =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"wss\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("nstart =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("25");#NormalTok(")");],));
