<fig-sleepstudy>


Two things are clear. Almost everyone gets slower as the days go by. And people differ: some start faster than others (different #strong[intercepts]), and some slow down more than others (different #strong[slopes]).

=== Random intercepts
<random-intercepts>
An ordinary regression would fit one line through all 180 points, as if they came from 180 different people. A #strong[random intercept model] fits one average line, but lets each person have their own starting level. In the formula, #NormalTok("(1 | Subject)"); means "a separate intercept for each subject", and #NormalTok("lmer()"); fits the model:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("sleep_ri ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lmer");#NormalTok("(Reaction ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" Days ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" Subject), ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" sleepstudy)");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(sleep_ri)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Linear mixed model fit by REML ['lmerMod']");],
[#NormalTok("Formula: Reaction ~ Days + (1 | Subject)");],
[#NormalTok("   Data: sleepstudy");],
[],
[#NormalTok("REML criterion at convergence: 1786.5");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Scaled residuals: ");],
[#NormalTok("    Min      1Q  Median      3Q     Max ");],
[#NormalTok("-3.2257 -0.5529  0.0109  0.5188  4.2506 ");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Random effects:");],
[#NormalTok(" Groups   Name        Variance Std.Dev.");],
[#NormalTok(" Subject  (Intercept) 1378.2   37.12   ");],
[#NormalTok(" Residual              960.5   30.99   ");],
[#NormalTok("Number of obs: 180, groups:  Subject, 18");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Fixed effects:");],
[#NormalTok("            Estimate Std. Error t value");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept) 251.4051     9.7467   25.79");],
[#NormalTok("Days         10.4673     0.8042   13.02");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Correlation of Fixed Effects:");],
[#NormalTok("     (Intr)");],
[#NormalTok("Days -0.371");],));
]
]
The output has two important parts. The #strong[fixed effects] describe the average line, as in ordinary regression: on day 0, the average reaction time is about 251 ms, and each day of sleep restriction adds about 10.5 ms. The #strong[random effects] describe how much people vary around the average line. The standard deviation of the intercepts, about 37 ms, says that people's starting levels typically differ from the average by that much; the residual standard deviation, about 31 ms, is the day-to-day variation within a person.

=== The intraclass correlation
<the-intraclass-correlation>
The random intercept model splits the variation in reaction times into two parts: stable differences between people, and variation within each person from day to day. The #strong[intraclass correlation] (ICC) compares them:

$ upright("ICC") = frac(upright("variance between groups"), upright("variance between groups") + upright("variance within groups")) $

The function #NormalTok("VarCorr()"); extracts the variances:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("vc ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("as.data.frame");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("VarCorr");#NormalTok("(sleep_ri))");],
[#NormalTok("vc");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("vcov[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("] ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sum");#NormalTok("(vc");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("vcov)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.5893089");],));
]
]
About 59% of the variation in reaction times is due to stable differences between people. The ICC can be read as a comparison of how much people differ from each other with how much each person varies. An ICC of 0 would mean the grouping does not matter: two measurements of the same person are no more alike than measurements of two different people. The higher the ICC, the more the measurements of one person repeat each other, and the more wrong an ordinary regression would be.

=== Random slopes
<random-slopes>
The random intercept model still assumes that everyone slows down at the same rate, which #ref(<fig-sleepstudy>, supplement: [Figure]) shows is not true. A #strong[random slope model] lets each person have their own slope as well. In the formula, #NormalTok("(Days | Subject)"); means "a separate intercept #emph[and] slope of #NormalTok("Days"); for each subject":

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("sleep_rs ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lmer");#NormalTok("(Reaction ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" Days ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (Days ");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" Subject), ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" sleepstudy)");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(sleep_rs)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("coefficients");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("             Estimate Std. Error   t value");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept) 251.40510   6.824597 36.838090");],
[#NormalTok("Days         10.46729   1.545790  6.771481");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("VarCorr");#NormalTok("(sleep_rs)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok(" Groups   Name        Std.Dev. Corr ");],
[#NormalTok(" Subject  (Intercept) 24.7407       ");],
[#NormalTok("          Days         5.9221  0.066");],
[#NormalTok(" Residual             25.5918       ");],));
]
]
The average effect of a day of sleep restriction is the same, about 10.5 ms, but its standard error has grown from 0.8 to 1.55. People's slopes vary with a standard deviation of about 5.9 ms per day, so for most people a day of sleep restriction adds somewhere between about 5 and 16 ms. Ignoring that variation made the random intercept model too confident about the average.

A #strong[likelihood ratio test], run with #NormalTok("anova()");, shows whether the random slope is worth adding:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("anova");#NormalTok("(sleep_ri, sleep_rs)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("refitting model(s) with ML (instead of REML)");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Data: sleepstudy");],
[#NormalTok("Models:");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_ri: Reaction ~ Days + (1 | Subject)");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_rs: Reaction ~ Days + (Days | Subject)");],
[#NormalTok("         npar    AIC    BIC  logLik -2*log(L)  Chisq Df Pr(>Chisq)    ");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_ri    4 1802.1 1814.8 -897.04    1794.1                         ");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_rs    6 1763.9 1783.1 -875.97    1751.9 42.139  2  7.072e-10 ***");],
[#NormalTok("---");],
[#NormalTok("Signif. codes:  0 '***' 0.001 '**' 0.01 '*' 0.05 '.' 0.1 ' ' 1");],));
]
]
The p-value is tiny: the random slopes improve the model clearly. The #strong[AIC] column agrees. AIC balances fit against complexity, and lower is better.

== Change in wellbeing over two years
<change-in-wellbeing-over-two-years>
The wellbeing study has up to four semester records for each student, joined with their background information. The variable #NormalTok("time"); counts semesters from the first, so that time 0 is semester 1:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("panel ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" semesters ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("left_join");#NormalTok("(students, ");#FunctionTok("join_by");#NormalTok("(student_id)) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("time =");#NormalTok(" semester ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")");],
[#FunctionTok("nrow");#NormalTok("(panel)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 2326");],));
]
]
#ref(<fig-spaghetti>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the wellbeing of 30 randomly chosen students across the four semesters.

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("9");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("some_students ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sample");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("unique");#NormalTok("(panel");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("student_id), ");#DecValTok("30");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("panel ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(student_id ");#SpecialCharTok("%in%");#NormalTok(" some_students) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" semester, ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" wellbeing, ");#AttributeTok("group =");#NormalTok(" student_id)) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_line");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("alpha =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.6");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("labs");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Semester\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Wellbeing (0 to 100)\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("theme_minimal");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("base_size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("13");#NormalTok(")");],));
#figure([
#box(image("10-mixed-models_files/figure-typst/fig-spaghetti-1.svg", alt: "Thirty thin lines across four semesters. The lines are spread widely, from about 30 to 90, and each stays at roughly its own level, with small ups and downs."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Wellbeing over four semesters for 30 randomly chosen students.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-spaghetti>


Students differ enormously in their overall level, much more than any student changes from one semester to the next. A model with no predictors, only a random intercept for each student, puts a number on this through the ICC:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("wb_null ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lmer");#NormalTok("(wellbeing ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" student_id), ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" panel)");],
[#NormalTok("vc_null ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("as.data.frame");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("VarCorr");#NormalTok("(wb_null))");],
[#NormalTok("vc_null");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("vcov[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("] ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sum");#NormalTok("(vc_null");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("vcov)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.7809117");],));
]
]
About 78% of all the variation in wellbeing is between students: students differ from each other far more than they change. The consequence for the amount of information in the data can be calculated. The #strong[design effect], $1 + \( m - 1 \) times upright("ICC")$, where $m$ is the number of records per student, says how many times more records are needed to give the same information as independent observations:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("icc ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" vc_null");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("vcov[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("] ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sum");#NormalTok("(vc_null");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("vcov)");],
[#NormalTok("records_per_student ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("nrow");#NormalTok("(panel) ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("n_distinct");#NormalTok("(panel");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("student_id)");],
[#NormalTok("design_effect ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (records_per_student ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("*");#NormalTok(" icc");],
[#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("records =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("nrow");#NormalTok("(panel), ");#AttributeTok("design_effect =");#NormalTok(" design_effect,");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("independent_equivalent =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("nrow");#NormalTok("(panel) ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" design_effect)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("               records          design_effect independent_equivalent ");],
[#NormalTok("           2326.000000               3.246423             716.480955 ");],));
]
]
The 2326 semester records carry about as much information about average wellbeing as 716 independent observations would. Treating them as 2326 independent records would therefore be seriously wrong.

=== Why ordinary regression misleads
<why-ordinary-regression-misleads>
The first question about change is whether wellbeing declines over time. The #emph[wrong] way to answer it is an ordinary regression that ignores the structure:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("lm");#NormalTok("(wellbeing ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" time, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" panel))");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("coefficients");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("              Estimate Std. Error    t value   Pr(>|t|)");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept) 61.6319636  0.4124650 149.423511 0.00000000");],
[#NormalTok("time        -0.3794867  0.2235408  -1.697618 0.08971384");],));
]
]
The slope is slightly negative, but not significant. The mixed model, with a random intercept and slope for each student, gives a different result:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("wb_growth ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lmer");#NormalTok("(wellbeing ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" time ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (time ");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" student_id), ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" panel)");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(wb_growth)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("coefficients");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("              Estimate Std. Error    t value");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept) 61.6455545  0.4764479 129.385717");],
[#NormalTok("time        -0.4342392  0.1107653  -3.920355");],));
]
]
The average slope is similar, about -0.43 points per semester, but its standard error has fallen from 0.22 to 0.11, and the decline is now clearly significant (a #emph[t] value of about -3.9). The reason is that the ordinary regression lumps the large, stable differences #emph[between] students into its error term, which drowns the small change #emph[within] each student. The mixed model separates the two, and sees the change clearly.

In the simulation at the start of the chapter, ignoring the structure made a non-existent effect look significant; in the sleep study, ignoring the differences in slopes made the average look more certain than it was; here, ignoring the structure makes a real decline look uncertain. The direction of the error depends on the data, but the error is always there.

=== Confidence intervals and p-values
<confidence-intervals-and-p-values>
The lme4 package deliberately prints no p-values for fixed effects, because calculating them exactly for mixed models is not straightforward. There are three common solutions. The first is to report #strong[confidence intervals], which are often more informative than p-values anyway. The function #NormalTok("confint()"); calculates them, and #NormalTok("method = \"Wald\""); is quick:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("confint");#NormalTok("(wb_growth, ");#AttributeTok("parm =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"beta_\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("method =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Wald\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                 2.5 %     97.5 %");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept) 60.7117337 62.5793752");],
[#NormalTok("time        -0.6513351 -0.2171432");],));
]
]
The argument #NormalTok("parm = \"beta_\""); asks for the fixed effects only. The interval for #NormalTok("time"); lies entirely below zero: wellbeing declines.

The second solution is a #strong[likelihood ratio test], comparing models with and without a term with #NormalTok("anova()");, as for the random slopes above. The third is the #strong[lmerTest] package, which adds p-values to the #NormalTok("summary()"); output and is used in most published research. Once it is loaded, #NormalTok("lmer()"); models fitted afterwards include a p-value for each fixed effect:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(lmerTest)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("Attaching package: 'lmerTest'");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("The following object is masked from 'package:lme4':");],
[],
[#NormalTok("    lmer");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("The following object is masked from 'package:stats':");],
[],
[#NormalTok("    step");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("wb_growth_p ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lmer");#NormalTok("(wellbeing ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" time ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (time ");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" student_id), ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" panel)");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(wb_growth_p)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("coefficients");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("              Estimate Std. Error       df    t value     Pr(>|t|)");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept) 61.6455545  0.4764479 598.9342 129.385717 0.000000e+00");],
[#NormalTok("time        -0.4342392  0.1107653 571.4997  -3.920355 9.914491e-05");],));
]
]
The new columns are the degrees of freedom (estimated by Satterthwaite's method) and the p-value, #NormalTok("Pr(>|t|)");. The decline over time is clearly significant, in agreement with the confidence interval. Chapter 5's hypothesis for RQ8, that wellbeing declines over the two years, is supported.

=== Persistence of the workshop effect
<persistence-of-the-workshop-effect>
Chapter 7 showed that the workshop raised wellbeing in semester 2. A mixed model can compare the two groups in every semester at once, using every record, including the first year of the students who later left. Treating #NormalTok("semester"); as a factor lets each semester have its own average, and the interaction with #NormalTok("workshop"); lets the gap between the groups differ from semester to semester:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("wb_workshop ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lmer");#NormalTok("(wellbeing ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("factor");#NormalTok("(semester) ");#SpecialCharTok("*");#NormalTok(" workshop ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (time ");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" student_id),");],
[#NormalTok("                    ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" panel)");],
[#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(wb_workshop)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("coefficients, ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                                      Estimate Std. Error      df t value");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept)                              60.65       0.69  667.20   87.89");],
[#NormalTok("factor(semester)2                         4.82       0.42 1452.39   11.38");],
[#NormalTok("factor(semester)3                         2.49       0.45 1595.59    5.51");],
[#NormalTok("factor(semester)4                         0.21       0.48  667.71    0.43");],
[#NormalTok("workshopNot invited                      -0.38       0.98  667.20   -0.39");],
[#NormalTok("factor(semester)2:workshopNot invited    -4.87       0.60 1452.39   -8.13");],
[#NormalTok("factor(semester)3:workshopNot invited    -3.75       0.64 1597.01   -5.85");],
[#NormalTok("factor(semester)4:workshopNot invited    -2.26       0.68  668.47   -3.30");],
[#NormalTok("                                      Pr(>|t|)");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept)                               0.00");],
[#NormalTok("factor(semester)2                         0.00");],
[#NormalTok("factor(semester)3                         0.00");],
[#NormalTok("factor(semester)4                         0.67");],
[#NormalTok("workshopNot invited                       0.70");],
[#NormalTok("factor(semester)2:workshopNot invited     0.00");],
[#NormalTok("factor(semester)3:workshopNot invited     0.00");],
[#NormalTok("factor(semester)4:workshopNot invited     0.00");],));
]
]
The coefficients are easier to understand as predicted averages. The function #NormalTok("predict()"); with #NormalTok("re.form = NA"); gives the predictions for the average student, ignoring the random effects:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("predicted ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("expand.grid");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("semester =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("workshop =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Invited\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Not invited\"");#NormalTok(")) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("time =");#NormalTok(" semester ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("predicted");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("wellbeing ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("predict");#NormalTok("(wb_workshop, ");#AttributeTok("newdata =");#NormalTok(" predicted, ");#AttributeTok("re.form =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("NA");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(predicted, ");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" semester, ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" wellbeing, ");#AttributeTok("colour =");#NormalTok(" workshop)) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_line");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("linewidth =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_point");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("size =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("2.5");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("scale_colour_viridis_d");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("end =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.8");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("labs");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Semester\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Predicted wellbeing\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("colour =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Workshop\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("theme_minimal");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("base_size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("13");#NormalTok(")");],));
#figure([
#box(image("10-mixed-models_files/figure-typst/fig-workshop-fade-1.svg", alt: "Two lines over semesters 1 to 4. They start together; the invited group rises about 5 points above the other in semester 2, and the gap narrows in semesters 3 and 4."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Predicted wellbeing in each semester by workshop group, from the mixed-effects model.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-workshop-fade>


The gap between the groups is 0.4 points in semester 1, before the workshop, 5.2 in semester 2, 4.1 in semester 3, and 2.6 in semester 4. The workshop's effect is real, but it fades over the following year. For the thesis's recommendations, that matters: a short follow-up session each year might keep the benefit going.

The interaction coefficients test whether the gap in each semester differs from the gap in semester 1. All three do (the largest p-value is 0.001).

=== Change in GPA
<change-in-gpa>
The same growth model can be fitted to GPA:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("gpa_growth ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lmer");#NormalTok("(gpa ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" time ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (time ");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" student_id), ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" panel)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("boundary (singular) fit: see help('isSingular')");],));
]
]
R warns that the fit is #strong[singular]. This message is common, and worth understanding. It means the model is more complex than the data supports: here, the students' GPA slopes hardly vary at all, so the model cannot estimate their spread. The solution is to simplify, keeping only the random intercept:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("gpa_growth ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lmer");#NormalTok("(gpa ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" time ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" student_id), ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" panel)");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(gpa_growth)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("coefficients");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                 Estimate  Std. Error        df     t value  Pr(>|t|)");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept)  3.0921628374 0.013015341  806.5702 237.5783101 0.0000000");],
[#NormalTok("time        -0.0004749256 0.003392086 1738.6474  -0.1400099 0.8886684");],));
]
]
The slope of #NormalTok("time"); is almost exactly zero: grades stay stable over the two years, even though wellbeing falls. A non-change is a finding too, and here an interesting one.

== Students within supervisors
<students-within-supervisors>
The students are also nested within supervisors, and a model can include random intercepts for both levels at once:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("wb_levels ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lmer");#NormalTok("(wellbeing ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" time ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" supervisor_id) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" student_id), ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" panel)");],
[#FunctionTok("VarCorr");#NormalTok("(wb_levels)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok(" Groups        Name        Std.Dev.");],
[#NormalTok(" student_id    (Intercept) 9.7748  ");],
[#NormalTok(" supervisor_id (Intercept) 4.4098  ");],
[#NormalTok(" Residual                  5.6189  ");],));
]
]
Because every student ID is unique, R knows that students are nested within supervisors. Dividing each variance by the total shows where the variation lies:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("vc_levels ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("as.data.frame");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("VarCorr");#NormalTok("(wb_levels))");],
[#FunctionTok("data.frame");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("level =");#NormalTok(" vc_levels");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("grp, ");#AttributeTok("share =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(vc_levels");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("vcov ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sum");#NormalTok("(vc_levels");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("vcov), ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("          level share");],
[#NormalTok("1    student_id  0.65");],
[#NormalTok("2 supervisor_id  0.13");],
[#NormalTok("3      Residual  0.22");],));
]
]
About 13% of all the variation in wellbeing lies between supervisors: students of the same supervisor are noticeably more alike. Most of the variation, 65%, lies between students within the same supervisor, and the remaining 22% is change within students from semester to semester. For the university, the supervisor share is a practical finding: who supervises a student makes a measurable difference to their wellbeing. It also means that any comparison of supervisors, or of anything assigned to supervisors, must allow for this grouping, as the simulation at the start of the chapter showed.

== Counts and yes-or-no outcomes
<counts-and-yes-or-no-outcomes>
Like ordinary regression, mixed models extend to other kinds of outcome. The function #NormalTok("glmer()"); fits a #strong[generalised linear mixed model]: #NormalTok("family = binomial"); for yes-or-no outcomes, as in Chapter 8's logistic regression, and #NormalTok("family = poisson"); for #strong[counts], such as the number of meetings a student had with their supervisor each semester.

The model below examines whether students who feel more supported meet their supervisor more often. Support is calculated from the questionnaire, as before:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("support_scores ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" questionnaire ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("support =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rowMeans");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("pick");#NormalTok("(support_1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#NormalTok("support_6), ");#AttributeTok("na.rm =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("select");#NormalTok("(student_id, support)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("meetings_model ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("glmer");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("  supervisor_meetings ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" support ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" programme ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" supervisor_id) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" student_id),");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" panel ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("left_join");#NormalTok("(support_scores, ");#FunctionTok("join_by");#NormalTok("(student_id)),");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("family =");#NormalTok(" poisson");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(meetings_model)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("coefficients");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("               Estimate Std. Error    z value     Pr(>|z|)");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept)  0.06224346 0.07134373  0.8724446 3.829659e-01");],
[#NormalTok("support      0.39258255 0.01975713 19.8704237 7.338090e-88");],
[#NormalTok("programmePhD 0.17676057 0.02881998  6.1332638 8.609423e-10");],));
]
]
Poisson coefficients are on a log scale, and #NormalTok("exp()"); turns them into #strong[rate ratios]:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("exp");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("fixef");#NormalTok("(meetings_model))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok(" (Intercept)      support programmePhD ");],
[#NormalTok("    1.064221     1.480800     1.193345 ");],));
]
]
Each extra point of support goes with about 48% more meetings per semester, and PhD students have about 19% more meetings than Master's students, holding support constant. As always with observational data, the direction is not certain: more meetings may also make students feel more supported.

