position: bottom, 
[
Prediction error (RMSE) on the training points and on new points, for polynomials of degree 1 to 12. Training error keeps falling as the model becomes more flexible; error on new data falls, then rises.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-bias-variance>


The training error falls steadily as the model becomes more flexible: a more flexible model can always fit its own data more closely. The error on new data falls at first, reaches its minimum at degree 3, and then rises. This U shape is known as the #strong[bias-variance trade-off]. A model that is too simple has high #strong[bias]: it misses part of the real pattern, whatever data it is given. A model that is too flexible has high #strong[variance]: it changes a great deal from one sample to another, because it follows each sample's noise. The best predictions come from a model in between, and the only way to find it is to measure performance on data the model did not learn from. The rest of the chapter builds that measurement into every step.

== Designing a fair test
<designing-a-fair-test>
The #strong[tidymodels] framework is a collection of packages that together make a fair test of a predictive model easy to carry out. Each package handles one part of the design: #strong[rsample] splits the data, #strong[recipes] prepares it, #strong[parsnip] specifies models, #strong[workflows] combines the pieces, #strong[tune] tunes models, and #strong[yardstick] measures performance. Loading #NormalTok("tidymodels"); loads them all:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(tidymodels)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("── Attaching packages ────────────────────────────────────── tidymodels 1.5.0 ──");],));
]
#block[
