[#NormalTok("✔ dials        1.4.4      ✔ tailor       0.1.0 ");],
[#NormalTok("✔ infer        1.1.0      ✔ tidyr        1.3.2 ");],
[#NormalTok("✔ modeldata    1.6.0      ✔ tune         2.1.0 ");],
[#NormalTok("✔ parsnip      1.6.0      ✔ workflows    1.3.0 ");],
[#NormalTok("✔ purrr        1.2.2      ✔ workflowsets 1.1.1 ");],
[#NormalTok("✔ recipes      1.4.0      ✔ yardstick    1.4.0 ");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("── Conflicts ───────────────────────────────────────── tidymodels_conflicts() ──");],
[#NormalTok("✖ purrr::discard()   masks scales::discard()");],
[#NormalTok("✖ dplyr::filter()    masks stats::filter()");],
[#NormalTok("✖ dplyr::lag()       masks stats::lag()");],
[#NormalTok("✖ .GlobalEnv::rmse() masks yardstick::rmse()");],
[#NormalTok("✖ recipes::step()    masks stats::step()");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("tidymodels_prefer");#NormalTok("()");],));
]
The function #NormalTok("tidymodels_prefer()"); settles a few name clashes between packages in favour of tidymodels. #ref(<fig-pipeline>, supplement: [Figure]) shows how the pieces fit together: the test data is set aside before anything else happens, all choices are made with the training data alone, and the test data is used once, at the end. The rest of the chapter works through each step.

