== Specifying and fitting a model
<specifying-and-fitting-a-model>
The #strong[parsnip] package specifies models in one consistent way, whatever package does the actual work. Here is logistic regression:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("logistic_spec ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("logistic_reg");#NormalTok("()");],
[#NormalTok("logistic_spec");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Logistic Regression Model Specification (classification)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Computational engine: glm ");],));
]
]
A #strong[workflow] combines the recipe and the model, so they always travel together:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("logistic_wf ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("workflow");#NormalTok("() ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("add_recipe");#NormalTok("(dropout_recipe) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("add_model");#NormalTok("(logistic_spec)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("logistic_fit ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("fit");#NormalTok("(logistic_wf, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" dropout_train)");],));
]
The function #NormalTok("fit()"); prepares the recipe on the training data and fits the model to the prepared data, in one step.

== Evaluating a classifier
<evaluating-a-classifier>
The model is now judged on the test students. The function #NormalTok("augment()"); adds the model's predictions to the test data: the predicted class (#NormalTok(".pred_class");) and the predicted probability of each class (#NormalTok(".pred_Yes");, #NormalTok(".pred_No");):

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("test_results ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("augment");#NormalTok("(logistic_fit, ");#AttributeTok("new_data =");#NormalTok(" dropout_test)");],
[#NormalTok("test_results ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("select");#NormalTok("(considering_dropout, .pred_class, .pred_Yes) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("head");#NormalTok("()");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 6 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  considering_dropout .pred_class .pred_Yes");],
[#NormalTok("  <fct>               <fct>           <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1 No                  No            0.216  ");],
[#NormalTok("2 No                  No            0.0279 ");],
[#NormalTok("3 No                  No            0.00744");],
[#NormalTok("4 Yes                 No            0.424  ");],
[#NormalTok("5 No                  No            0.0114 ");],
[#NormalTok("6 No                  No            0.0298 ");],));
]
]
The simplest measure is #strong[accuracy]: the share of students classified correctly. The #strong[yardstick] package calculates it:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("test_results ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("accuracy");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("truth =");#NormalTok(" considering_dropout, ");#AttributeTok("estimate =");#NormalTok(" .pred_class)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 1 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric  .estimator .estimate");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>    <chr>          <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1 accuracy binary         0.834");],));
]
]
An accuracy of 83% sounds good. But a "model" that ignores every predictor and says "No" for every student would be right 85% of the time, because most students answer "No", while finding not one of the students at risk. For an imbalanced outcome, accuracy is a misleading measure.

A better measure is the #strong[ROC AUC] (the area under the ROC curve). It is the probability that, of one student who considered dropping out and one who did not, the model gives the first a higher predicted probability than the second. An AUC of 0.5 is no better than a coin toss, and 1.0 is perfect. Unlike accuracy, it is not fooled by an imbalanced outcome. It uses the predicted probabilities:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("test_results ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("roc_auc");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("truth =");#NormalTok(" considering_dropout, .pred_Yes)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 1 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric .estimator .estimate");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>   <chr>          <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1 roc_auc binary         0.840");],));
]
]
An AUC of 0.84 means the model ranks an at-risk student above a not-at-risk student about 84% of the time: a useful model. Chapter 12 looks at classification measures in much more detail, including how to choose the threshold at which a student is flagged.

== Overfitting in practice
<overfitting-in-practice>
The simulation at the start of the chapter showed overfitting with a flexible curve. The same happens with real data. A dramatic example is #strong[k-nearest neighbours] (k-NN) with $k = 1$. It predicts each student's outcome by finding the single most similar student in the training data and copying their answer. On the training data, every student's nearest neighbour is themselves, so it can never be wrong:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("knn1_wf ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("workflow");#NormalTok("() ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("add_recipe");#NormalTok("(dropout_recipe) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("add_model");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("nearest_neighbor");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("neighbors =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("set_mode");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"classification\"");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("knn1_fit ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("fit");#NormalTok("(knn1_wf, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" dropout_train)");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("augment");#NormalTok("(knn1_fit, ");#AttributeTok("new_data =");#NormalTok(" dropout_train) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("roc_auc");#NormalTok("(considering_dropout, .pred_Yes)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 1 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric .estimator .estimate");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>   <chr>          <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1 roc_auc binary             1");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("augment");#NormalTok("(knn1_fit, ");#AttributeTok("new_data =");#NormalTok(" dropout_test) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("roc_auc");#NormalTok("(considering_dropout, .pred_Yes)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 1 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric .estimator .estimate");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>   <chr>          <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1 roc_auc binary         0.671");],));
]
]
The model scores perfectly on the training data, and much more poorly on new students. The training score was an illusion: the model had memorised the training students, not learned anything that carries over. It is the degree-12 curve again, and the reason every model in this book is tested on data it has not seen.

== Cross-validation
<cross-validation>
The test set is used only once, at the end. During model building, however, models and settings often need to be compared, and neither the training data (that would reward overfitting) nor the test set (that would use it up) can be used to judge them. #strong[Cross-validation] solves this by reusing the training data. The training data is split into, say, 10 equal parts, called #strong[folds]. The model is fitted on 9 folds and its performance measured on the fold left out; this is repeated 10 times, leaving out each fold in turn, and the 10 performance measures are averaged. Every student is used for evaluation exactly once, always by a model that did not see them during fitting. #ref(<fig-folds>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the idea with 5 folds.

#figure([
#box(image("11-machine-learning_files/figure-typst/fig-folds-1.svg", alt: "A grid of five rows, one per round, each split into five blocks. In each row, a different block is dark, marking the fold used for evaluation; the other four are grey."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Five-fold cross-validation: in each round, the model is fitted on four folds (grey) and evaluated on the fifth (dark).
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-folds>


The function #NormalTok("vfold_cv()"); creates the folds, and #NormalTok("fit_resamples()"); fits and evaluates the workflow on each:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("dropout_folds ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("vfold_cv");#NormalTok("(dropout_train, ");#AttributeTok("v =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("strata =");#NormalTok(" considering_dropout)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("logistic_cv ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("fit_resamples");#NormalTok("(logistic_wf, ");#AttributeTok("resamples =");#NormalTok(" dropout_folds,");],
[#NormalTok("                             ");#AttributeTok("metrics =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("metric_set");#NormalTok("(roc_auc, accuracy))");],
[#FunctionTok("collect_metrics");#NormalTok("(logistic_cv)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 2 × 6");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric  .estimator  mean     n std_err .config        ");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>    <chr>      <dbl> <int>   <dbl> <chr>          ");],
[#NormalTok("1 accuracy binary     0.862    10 0.00806 pre0_mod0_post0");],
[#NormalTok("2 roc_auc  binary     0.804    10 0.0303  pre0_mod0_post0");],));
]
]
The cross-validated AUC, about 0.8, is an honest estimate of how the model will perform on new students, obtained without touching the test set. The standard error (#NormalTok("std_err");) shows how much the estimate varies across folds.

== Tuning a model
<tuning-a-model>
Many models have settings that are not learned from the data but must be chosen beforehand, such as $k$ in k-nearest neighbours or the degree of the polynomials in the simulation. They are called #strong[hyperparameters], to distinguish them from the #strong[parameters], such as regression coefficients, that the model learns. The best value is found by #strong[tuning]: trying several values and comparing them with cross-validation.

A #strong[decision tree] (explained fully in Chapter 12) predicts by asking a series of yes-or-no questions, such as whether stress is above 3.5. Its depth, the number of questions in a row, is a hyperparameter that controls its flexibility. A shallow tree is too simple to capture the patterns and underfits; a very deep tree can fit the training data closely, noise included, and overfits. In the model specification, #NormalTok("tune()"); marks the hyperparameter to be tuned:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("tree_spec ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("decision_tree");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("tree_depth =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("tune");#NormalTok("(), ");#AttributeTok("min_n =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("cost_complexity =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("set_mode");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"classification\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("tree_wf ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("workflow");#NormalTok("() ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("add_recipe");#NormalTok("(dropout_recipe) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("add_model");#NormalTok("(tree_spec)");],));
]
The function #NormalTok("tune_grid()"); tries each value in a grid, using the same cross-validation folds:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("tree_tuning ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("tune_grid");#NormalTok("(tree_wf, ");#AttributeTok("resamples =");#NormalTok(" dropout_folds,");],
[#NormalTok("                         ");#AttributeTok("grid =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("tibble");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("tree_depth =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("                         ");#AttributeTok("metrics =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("metric_set");#NormalTok("(roc_auc))");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("autoplot");#NormalTok("(tree_tuning) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("theme_minimal");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("base_size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("12");#NormalTok(")");],));
#figure([
#box(image("11-machine-learning_files/figure-typst/fig-tuning-1.svg", alt: "Line chart of AUC against tree depth from 1 to 10. The AUC rises steeply from 0.5 at depth 1 to a peak at a moderate depth, then levels off and dips slightly for deeper trees."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Cross-validated ROC AUC of decision trees of different depths.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-tuning>


The pattern is the new-data curve of #ref(<fig-bias-variance>, supplement: [Figure]) seen from the other side: a tree of depth 1 is no better than chance, the AUC rises as the tree grows, and beyond a moderate depth it levels off and dips slightly as the deeper trees start to overfit. The function #NormalTok("select_best()"); picks the best depth, here 4:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("best_depth ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("select_best");#NormalTok("(tree_tuning, ");#AttributeTok("metric =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"roc_auc\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("best_depth");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 1 × 2");],
[#NormalTok("  tree_depth .config         ");],
[#NormalTok("       <int> <chr>           ");],
[#NormalTok("1          4 pre0_mod04_post0");],));
]
]
== The final test
<the-final-test>
Only now, with the model chosen, is the test set used. The function #NormalTok("finalize_workflow()"); plugs the best depth into the workflow, and #NormalTok("last_fit()"); fits it on the whole training set and evaluates it once on the test set:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("final_tree ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" tree_wf ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("finalize_workflow");#NormalTok("(best_depth) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("last_fit");#NormalTok("(dropout_split, ");#AttributeTok("metrics =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("metric_set");#NormalTok("(roc_auc, accuracy))");],
[#FunctionTok("collect_metrics");#NormalTok("(final_tree)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 2 × 4");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric  .estimator .estimate .config        ");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>    <chr>          <dbl> <chr>          ");],
[#NormalTok("1 accuracy binary         0.828 pre0_mod0_post0");],
[#NormalTok("2 roc_auc  binary         0.815 pre0_mod0_post0");],));
]
]
The same is done for logistic regression, for comparison:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("final_logistic ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("last_fit");#NormalTok("(logistic_wf, dropout_split, ");#AttributeTok("metrics =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("metric_set");#NormalTok("(roc_auc, accuracy))");],
[#FunctionTok("collect_metrics");#NormalTok("(final_logistic)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 2 × 4");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric  .estimator .estimate .config        ");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>    <chr>          <dbl> <chr>          ");],
[#NormalTok("1 accuracy binary         0.834 pre0_mod0_post0");],
[#NormalTok("2 roc_auc  binary         0.840 pre0_mod0_post0");],));
]
]
The tuned tree reaches an AUC of 0.81; plain logistic regression, 0.84. The more flexible model is not the better one. In this data, the risk of dropout rises steadily with stress and falls steadily with support, so a simple smooth model captures the pattern well, while a tree, which splits the data into boxes, captures it less well. Trying a simple model first, and making a complex one earn its place, is one of the most useful habits in machine learning.

