== Decision trees
<decision-trees>
A #strong[decision tree] classifies by asking a series of yes-or-no questions, like a flowchart. To see how a tree chooses its questions, take twelve imaginary students:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("tiny ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("tibble");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("stress  =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("1.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.2");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.8");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.1");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.4");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.6");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.8");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.2");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.5");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("support =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("4.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.2");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.8");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.1");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.4");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("1.8");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.9");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("1.5");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("dropout =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("factor");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"No\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"No\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"No\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"No\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"No\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"No\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("                     ");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"No\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"No\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok(")");],));
]
Four of the twelve consider dropping out. The tree looks for the single question that best separates the "Yes" students from the "No" students. "Is support below 2.75?" puts seven students, all "No", on one side, and five students, four "Yes" and one "No", on the other. The one "No" student among the low-support group has the lowest stress, so a second question, "Is stress 3.1 or more?", separates the rest perfectly:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(rpart)");],
[#NormalTok("tiny_tree ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rpart");#NormalTok("(dropout ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" stress ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" support, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" tiny, ");#AttributeTok("method =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"class\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("                   ");#AttributeTok("control =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rpart.control");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("minsplit =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("cp =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("tiny_tree");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("n= 12 ");],
[],
[#NormalTok("node), split, n, loss, yval, (yprob)");],
[#NormalTok("      * denotes terminal node");],
[],
[#NormalTok("1) root 12 4 No (0.6666667 0.3333333)  ");],
[#NormalTok("  2) support>=2.75 7 0 No (1.0000000 0.0000000) *");],
[#NormalTok("  3) support< 2.75 5 1 Yes (0.2000000 0.8000000)  ");],
[#NormalTok("    6) stress< 3.1 1 0 No (1.0000000 0.0000000) *");],
[#NormalTok("    7) stress>=3.1 4 0 Yes (0.0000000 1.0000000) *");],));
]
]
Each line is a #strong[node]: the question that led to it, the number of students, the number misclassified, and the predicted class. The final nodes, marked #NormalTok("*");, are the #strong[leaves]. To classify a new student, you follow the questions from the top until you reach a leaf. To choose each question, the tree tries every predictor and every possible cut-off and picks the one that makes the two groups most "pure", usually measured by the #strong[Gini impurity] (0 when a group contains only one class).

Decision trees have attractive properties: they are easy to explain, they need no dummy variables or normalisation (a question such as "is stress 3.1 or more?" works the same on any scale), and they capture interactions automatically (stress matters here only for low-support students). For the wellbeing data, a recipe that only fills in missing values is enough:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("tree_recipe ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("recipe");#NormalTok("(considering_dropout ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ., ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" dropout_train) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("step_impute_median");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("all_numeric_predictors");#NormalTok("())");],
[],
[#NormalTok("tree_wf ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("workflow");#NormalTok("() ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("add_recipe");#NormalTok("(tree_recipe) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("add_model");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("decision_tree");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("tree_depth =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("min_n =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("set_mode");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"classification\"");#NormalTok("))");],
[],
[#NormalTok("tree_fit ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("fit");#NormalTok("(tree_wf, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" dropout_train)");],));
]
A depth of 3 keeps the tree small enough to read. #ref(<fig-tree>, supplement: [Figure]) draws it with #NormalTok("plot()"); and #NormalTok("text()"); from rpart; the #strong[rpart.plot] package draws nicer trees if you install it.

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("tree_engine ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("extract_fit_engine");#NormalTok("(tree_fit)");],
[#FunctionTok("plot");#NormalTok("(tree_engine, ");#AttributeTok("uniform =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("margin =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.08");#NormalTok(")");],
[#FunctionTok("text");#NormalTok("(tree_engine, ");#AttributeTok("use.n =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("cex =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.75");#NormalTok(")");],));
#figure([
#box(image("12-classification_files/figure-typst/fig-tree-1.svg", alt: "A tree diagram. The top split is on support. Further splits on stress and other scale scores lead to leaves labelled Yes or No, each with the number of Yes and No students in it."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
A decision tree of depth 3 for considering dropout. At each split, students who meet the condition go left.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-tree>


The first question is about support, the strongest single predictor. The numbers under each leaf are the training students in it (Yes/No). The tree is easy to read, but a single tree has a serious weakness: it is unstable. A slightly different sample of students can produce a completely different tree, and, as Chapter 11 showed, a deep tree overfits. The next method turns this weakness into a strength.

== Random forests
<random-forests>
A #strong[random forest] grows hundreds of trees, each on a slightly different version of the data, and lets them vote. Two sources of randomness make the trees different. Each tree is grown on a #strong[bootstrap sample] of the training data (Chapter 7), students drawn at random with replacement. And at each split, the tree may only choose from a random subset of the predictors; the size of this subset, #NormalTok("mtry");, is a hyperparameter.

Each individual tree overfits in its own way, but because the trees differ, many of their errors cancel out when the trees vote. The predicted probability of "Yes" is the share of trees that vote "Yes". Averaging many models in this way is called an #strong[ensemble]\; a random forest is an ensemble of trees @breiman2001.

The #strong[ranger] package fits random forests quickly. #NormalTok("importance = \"permutation\""); asks it to also measure how much each predictor matters:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("forest_spec ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rand_forest");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("trees =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("500");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("set_engine");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"ranger\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("importance =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"permutation\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("set_mode");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"classification\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("forest_wf ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("workflow");#NormalTok("() ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("add_recipe");#NormalTok("(tree_recipe) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("add_model");#NormalTok("(forest_spec)");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("forest_fit ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("fit");#NormalTok("(forest_wf, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" dropout_train)");],));
]
A forest of 500 trees cannot be drawn, so it is a "black box". #strong[Permutation importance] opens it a little: for each predictor in turn, the values are shuffled randomly, and the drop in the model's accuracy is recorded. Shuffling an important predictor hurts the predictions; shuffling an unimportant one does not. #ref(<fig-importance>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the result.

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("importance ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("extract_fit_engine");#NormalTok("(forest_fit)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("variable.importance");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("tibble");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("predictor =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("names");#NormalTok("(importance), ");#AttributeTok("importance =");#NormalTok(" importance) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" importance, ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("reorder");#NormalTok("(predictor, importance))) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_col");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("fill =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"#2f6793\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("labs");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Permutation importance (drop in accuracy)\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("NULL");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("theme_minimal");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("base_size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("12");#NormalTok(")");],));
#figure([
#box(image("12-classification_files/figure-typst/fig-importance-1.svg", alt: "Horizontal bar chart of predictor importance. Support and stress have the longest bars, followed by burnout; the demographic variables have bars near zero."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Permutation importance of each predictor in the random forest.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-importance>


The forest relies most on support, stress, and burnout, and hardly at all on the students' background, which agrees with the logistic regression of Chapter 8. Importance says which predictors the model #emph[uses], not what #emph[causes] dropout; everything Chapter 8 said about confounding still applies.

== k-nearest neighbours
<k-nearest-neighbours>
#strong[k-nearest neighbours] (k-NN), met briefly in Chapter 11, classifies a new student by finding the $k$ most similar students in the training data and letting them vote. "Similar" means close together when the predictors are drawn as a map. Suppose a new student in the tiny example has a stress score of 3.5 and a support score of 3.0. The #strong[distance] to each training student is the straight-line distance on the stress-support map:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("tiny ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("distance =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sqrt");#NormalTok("((stress ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("3.5");#NormalTok(")");#SpecialCharTok("^");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (support ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("3.0");#NormalTok(")");#SpecialCharTok("^");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("arrange");#NormalTok("(distance) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("head");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 3 × 4");],
[#NormalTok("  stress support dropout distance");],
[#NormalTok("   <dbl>   <dbl> <fct>      <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1    4       2.5 Yes        0.707");],
[#NormalTok("2    3.1     3.8 No         0.894");],
[#NormalTok("3    3.4     2.1 Yes        0.906");],));
]
]
With $k = 3$, two of the three nearest students considered dropping out, so the predicted probability of "Yes" is 2/3. #ref(<fig-knn>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the picture.

#figure([
#box(image("12-classification_files/figure-typst/fig-knn-1.svg", alt: "Scatter plot of support against stress for twelve students, coloured Yes or No. A cross marks a new student at stress 3.5 and support 3. A circle around the cross encloses three students: two Yes and one No."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
k-nearest neighbours with k = 3. The new student (cross) is classified by the three closest students (circled).
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-knn>


Because k-NN is based on distances, the predictors must be on the same scale. Otherwise a variable measured in large numbers, such as caffeine in milligrams, would dominate the distance, and a variable measured on a 1-5 scale would hardly count. That is why the recipe normalises every predictor.

The number of neighbours, $k$, is a hyperparameter. With $k = 1$, the model copies the nearest student and overfits badly (Chapter 11); with a large $k$, it averages over many students and becomes smoother. Tuning shows which works best for the wellbeing data:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("knn_wf ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("workflow");#NormalTok("() ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("add_recipe");#NormalTok("(dropout_recipe) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("add_model");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("nearest_neighbor");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("neighbors =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("tune");#NormalTok("()) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("set_mode");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"classification\"");#NormalTok("))");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("knn_tuning ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("tune_grid");#NormalTok("(knn_wf, ");#AttributeTok("resamples =");#NormalTok(" dropout_folds,");],
[#NormalTok("                        ");#AttributeTok("grid =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("tibble");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("neighbors =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("5");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("11");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("21");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("41");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("81");#NormalTok(")),");],
[#NormalTok("                        ");#AttributeTok("metrics =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("metric_set");#NormalTok("(roc_auc))");],
[#FunctionTok("collect_metrics");#NormalTok("(knn_tuning) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("select");#NormalTok("(neighbors, mean, std_err)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 6 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  neighbors  mean std_err");],
[#NormalTok("      <dbl> <dbl>   <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1         1 0.525  0.0200");],
[#NormalTok("2         5 0.623  0.0222");],
[#NormalTok("3        11 0.673  0.0256");],
[#NormalTok("4        21 0.708  0.0266");],
[#NormalTok("5        41 0.734  0.0286");],
[#NormalTok("6        81 0.746  0.0303");],));
]
]
The AUC keeps rising up to the largest $k$ tried. When the best value lies at the edge of the grid, the usual advice is to extend the grid, but here the result is informative in itself: the more neighbours k-NN averages over, the smoother its predictions become, and the better it does. The wellbeing data has a smooth pattern (risk rises steadily with stress and falls steadily with support), which a smooth model such as logistic regression captures directly. k-NN is at its best when the pattern is irregular and there is a lot of data.

== Support vector machines
<support-vector-machines>
A #strong[support vector machine] (SVM) separates the two classes with a boundary that is as far as possible from the students on either side. Imagine drawing a line between the "Yes" and "No" points on the stress-support map: of all the lines that separate them, the SVM picks the one with the widest empty "street" around it, the #strong[margin]. Only the students closest to the boundary, the #strong[support vectors], determine where it goes. When the classes overlap, as they always do in real data, some students are allowed inside the margin or on the wrong side, at a cost set by a hyperparameter.

A straight boundary is a #strong[linear SVM]. With a #strong[kernel], the SVM can draw curved boundaries: the popular #strong[radial basis function] (RBF) kernel lets the boundary bend around groups of points. Both are available in tidymodels through the #strong[kernlab] package:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("svm_linear_spec ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("svm_linear");#NormalTok("() ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("set_engine");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"kernlab\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("set_mode");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"classification\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("svm_rbf_spec ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("svm_rbf");#NormalTok("() ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("set_mode");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"classification\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
]
Like k-NN, SVMs are based on distances, so they need normalised predictors. #ref(<fig-boundaries>, supplement: [Figure]) shows how differently the four families draw their boundaries, using just stress and support so the result can be drawn as a map.

#figure([
#box(image("12-classification_files/figure-typst/fig-boundaries-1.svg", alt: "Four panels, each a map of stress (x) against support (y), shaded from blue (low risk) to orange (high risk). Logistic regression gives a smooth diagonal gradient; the decision tree gives three rectangular blocks; k-nearest neighbours gives a patchy, irregular surface; the RBF support vector machine gives smooth, rounded regions. In all four, risk is highest at high stress and low support."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Predicted probability of considering dropout from stress and support, according to four models fitted to the training data. Darker orange means higher risk; the points are the training students.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-boundaries>


