== Measuring prediction error
<measuring-prediction-error>
The quality of a numeric prediction is judged by its errors. Five students whose final GPA a model has predicted show how:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("tiny ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("tibble");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("actual    =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("3.2");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.8");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.6");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.5");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("predicted =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("3.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.9");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.3");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("3.1");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.9");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("tiny ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("error =");#NormalTok(" actual ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" predicted)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 5 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  actual predicted  error");],
[#NormalTok("   <dbl>     <dbl>  <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1    3.2       3    0.200");],
[#NormalTok("2    2.8       2.9 -0.100");],
[#NormalTok("3    3.6       3.3  0.300");],
[#NormalTok("4    3         3.1 -0.100");],
[#NormalTok("5    2.5       2.9 -0.4  ");],));
]
]
Each #strong[error] (or residual) is the actual value minus the prediction, and three measures summarise them. The #strong[MAE] (mean absolute error) is the average size of the errors, ignoring their sign; here, (0.2 + 0.1 + 0.3 + 0.1 + 0.4) / 5 = 0.22 GPA points. The #strong[RMSE] (root mean squared error) squares the errors, averages them, and takes the square root. Squaring gives large errors more weight, so the RMSE is always at least as large as the MAE, and much larger when there are a few big misses. #strong[R²], in yardstick, is the squared correlation between the predictions and the actual values: as in Chapter 8, the share of the variation in the outcome that the predictions capture, from 0 to 1. The yardstick package calculates all three:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("regression_metrics ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("metric_set");#NormalTok("(rmse, mae, rsq)");],
[#NormalTok("tiny ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("regression_metrics");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("truth =");#NormalTok(" actual, ");#AttributeTok("estimate =");#NormalTok(" predicted)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 3 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric .estimator .estimate");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>   <chr>          <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1 rmse    standard       0.249");],
[#NormalTok("2 mae     standard       0.220");],
[#NormalTok("3 rsq     standard       0.785");],));
]
]
MAE and RMSE are in the units of the outcome, here GPA points, which makes them easy to explain: "the model's predictions are typically off by about 0.2 GPA points". Lower is better. R² has no units; higher is better.

A number on its own means little, so every model should be compared with a #strong[baseline]: the simplest possible prediction. For a numeric outcome, the baseline predicts the same value, the training mean, for everyone, which is what parsnip's #NormalTok("null_model()"); does:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("null_wf ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("workflow");#NormalTok("(gpa_recipe, ");#FunctionTok("null_model");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("mode =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"regression\"");#NormalTok("))");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("null_cv ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("fit_resamples");#NormalTok("(null_wf, ");#AttributeTok("resamples =");#NormalTok(" gpa_folds, ");#AttributeTok("metrics =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("metric_set");#NormalTok("(rmse, mae))");],
[#FunctionTok("collect_metrics");#NormalTok("(null_cv)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 2 × 6");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric .estimator  mean     n std_err .config        ");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>   <chr>      <dbl> <int>   <dbl> <chr>          ");],
[#NormalTok("1 mae     standard   0.256    10 0.00738 pre0_mod0_post0");],
[#NormalTok("2 rmse    standard   0.323    10 0.0104  pre0_mod0_post0");],));
]
]
Predicting the average GPA for everyone gives an RMSE of 0.32. Any useful model must do clearly better. (The R² of the baseline cannot be calculated, because its predictions do not vary, so it is left out here.)

== Ordinary regression with many predictors
<ordinary-regression-with-many-predictors>
The obvious model is the multiple regression of Chapter 8, using every predictor:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("lm_wf ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("workflow");#NormalTok("(gpa_recipe, ");#FunctionTok("linear_reg");#NormalTok("())");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("lm_cv ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("fit_resamples");#NormalTok("(lm_wf, ");#AttributeTok("resamples =");#NormalTok(" gpa_folds, ");#AttributeTok("metrics =");#NormalTok(" regression_metrics)");],
[#FunctionTok("collect_metrics");#NormalTok("(lm_cv)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 3 × 6");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric .estimator  mean     n std_err .config        ");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>   <chr>      <dbl> <int>   <dbl> <chr>          ");],
[#NormalTok("1 mae     standard   0.172    10 0.00615 pre0_mod0_post0");],
[#NormalTok("2 rmse    standard   0.218    10 0.0105  pre0_mod0_post0");],
[#NormalTok("3 rsq     standard   0.564    10 0.0420  pre0_mod0_post0");],));
]
]
A cross-validated RMSE of 0.218, much better than the baseline. With 422 training students and 51 predictors, ordinary regression works reasonably well. But watch what happens with fewer students. Many theses have samples of 50 or 60, so here is the same model fitted to 60 randomly chosen training students, and tested on the test set:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("small_train ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" gpa_train ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("slice_sample");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("n =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("60");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("lm_small ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("fit");#NormalTok("(lm_wf, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" small_train)");],
[#FunctionTok("augment");#NormalTok("(lm_small, ");#AttributeTok("new_data =");#NormalTok(" small_train) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("regression_metrics");#NormalTok("(final_gpa, .pred)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 3 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric .estimator .estimate");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>   <chr>          <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1 rmse    standard      0.115 ");],
[#NormalTok("2 mae     standard      0.0918");],
[#NormalTok("3 rsq     standard      0.876 ");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("augment");#NormalTok("(lm_small, ");#AttributeTok("new_data =");#NormalTok(" gpa_test) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("regression_metrics");#NormalTok("(final_gpa, .pred)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 3 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric .estimator .estimate");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>   <chr>          <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1 rmse    standard      0.526 ");],
[#NormalTok("2 mae     standard      0.430 ");],
[#NormalTok("3 rsq     standard      0.0456");],));
]
]
On its own 60 students, the model looks excellent: an RMSE of 0.11. On new students, its RMSE is 0.53, #emph[worse than predicting the average GPA for everyone]. With 51 coefficients estimated from 60 students, the model has enough freedom to fit the noise in its sample, and the coefficients that fit the noise do not carry over. In the extreme, with as many predictors as students, a regression can fit its data perfectly and predict nothing.

The problem is made worse by #strong[multicollinearity]: many of the predictors are strongly correlated (six stress items, two semesters of GPA), and regression struggles to divide the credit between correlated predictors. Their coefficients become large, unstable, and sometimes of opposite signs, cancelling each other out on the training data but not on new data.

== Regularised regression
<regularised-regression>
#strong[Regularisation] tackles this directly: it fits the regression while penalising large coefficients. Ordinary regression chooses the coefficients that minimise the sum of squared errors. Regularised regression minimises

$ upright("sum of squared errors") + lambda times upright("size of the coefficients") . $

The #strong[penalty] $lambda$ (lambda) sets how much large coefficients cost. With $lambda = 0$, this is ordinary regression; as $lambda$ grows, the coefficients are pulled, or #strong[shrunk], towards zero. A little shrinkage costs almost nothing in fit to the training data, but makes the model much more stable on new data. Because the penalty depends on the size of the coefficients, the predictors must be on the same scale, which the recipe's #NormalTok("step_normalize()"); ensures. There are three versions, which differ in how "size" is measured. #strong[Ridge regression] uses the sum of the #emph[squared] coefficients: it shrinks all coefficients towards zero and shares the credit among correlated predictors, but keeps every predictor in the model. The #strong[lasso] (least absolute shrinkage and selection operator) uses the sum of the #emph[absolute] coefficients @tibshirani1996. It shrinks too, but it also sets some coefficients to exactly zero, removing those predictors, so it also #emph[selects] predictors and leaves a simpler model. The #strong[elastic net] mixes the two, with a #strong[mixture] parameter that runs from 0 (pure ridge) to 1 (pure lasso).

In tidymodels, all three are #NormalTok("linear_reg()"); with the #strong[glmnet] engine, with #NormalTok("penalty"); for $lambda$ and #NormalTok("mixture"); for the mix. The 60 students are now refitted with a lasso:

