=== A small example
<a-small-example-3>
A complete Quarto document can be very short:

#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("---");],
[#AnnotationTok("title:");#CommentTok(" \"Three friends' sleep\"");],
[#AnnotationTok("author:");#CommentTok(" \"Elaf\"");],
[#AnnotationTok("format:");#CommentTok(" html");],
[#CommentTok("---");],
[],
[#NormalTok("My three friends slept ");#InformationTok("`r mean(sleep)`");#NormalTok(" hours on average last night.");],
[],
[#InformationTok("```{r}");],
[#NormalTok("sleep ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("6.5");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("5.5");#NormalTok(")");],
[#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(sleep)");],
[#InformationTok("```");],));
It has the three parts of every Quarto document. The #strong[YAML header], between the two #NormalTok("---"); lines, holds settings: the title, the author, and the output #strong[format]. The #strong[text] is written in #strong[Markdown], a simple way of marking formatting: #NormalTok("**bold**");, #NormalTok("*italic*");, #NormalTok("# Heading");, and #NormalTok("-"); for a bullet point. The #strong[code chunks], between #NormalTok("```{r}"); and #NormalTok("```");, are run when the document is rendered, and their code and output appear in the document.

The text also contains #strong[inline code], #NormalTok("r mean(sleep)");: R code inside a sentence, written between backticks and starting with the letter r and a space. When the document is rendered, the inline code is replaced by its result, so the sentence reads "My three friends slept 6.3333333 hours on average last night." (Rounding it, with #NormalTok("round(mean(sleep), 1)");, would be better.) If a friend's number changes, the sentence changes with it. Inline code is the cure for the problem at the start of the chapter: numbers in the text are never typed by hand.

In RStudio, create a Quarto document with #emph[File \> New File \> Quarto Document], and render it with the #strong[Render] button. RStudio also offers a #strong[visual editor], which shows the formatting as in a word processor while writing the same #NormalTok(".qmd"); file underneath.

=== What happens when you render
<what-happens-when-you-render>
#ref(<fig-render>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the steps. The #strong[knitr] package runs the R code and writes a Markdown file with the results in place; #strong[Pandoc], a universal document converter, then turns the Markdown into the requested format.

#figure([
#block[
#box(image("17-reproducible-research_files\\figure-typst\\mermaid-figure-1.png", height: 6.06in, width: 4.16in))

]
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
How a Quarto document is rendered.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-render>


Because the code is run from the beginning every time, a rendered document is a test of reproducibility in itself: if the code depends on something that is not in the document or the project, such as an object created by hand in the Console, rendering fails.

== A results chapter in Quarto
<a-results-chapter-in-quarto>
The descriptive results chapter of the thesis, rewritten as a Quarto document, begins like this:

#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("---");],
[#AnnotationTok("title:");#CommentTok(" \"Chapter 4: Results\"");],
[#AnnotationTok("author:");#CommentTok(" \"Elaf\"");],
[#AnnotationTok("format:");#CommentTok(" docx");],
[#AnnotationTok("bibliography:");#CommentTok(" references.bib");],
[#AnnotationTok("execute:");],
[#CommentTok("  echo: false");],
[#CommentTok("---");],
[],
[#InformationTok("```{r}");],
[#CommentTok("#| label: setup");],
[#CommentTok("#| message: false");],
[#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(dplyr)");],
[#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(ggplot2)");],
[#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(data2thesis)");],
[#InformationTok("```");],
[],
[#NormalTok("The sample included ");#InformationTok("`r nrow(students)`");#NormalTok(" graduate students from");],
[#InformationTok("`r n_distinct(students$faculty)`");#NormalTok(" faculties. Their average wellbeing in the");],
[#NormalTok("first semester was ");#InformationTok("`r round(mean(semesters$wellbeing[semesters$semester == 1], na.rm = TRUE), 1)`");],
[#NormalTok("on the wellbeing index (0 to 100). @tbl-faculty shows wellbeing by faculty.");],
[],
[#InformationTok("```{r}");],
[#CommentTok("#| label: tbl-faculty");],
[#CommentTok("#| tbl-cap: \"Wellbeing in the first semester, by faculty.\"");],
[#NormalTok("semesters ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(semester ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("left_join");#NormalTok("(students, ");#FunctionTok("join_by");#NormalTok("(student_id)) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("summarise");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("Students =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("n");#NormalTok("(),");],
[#NormalTok("            ");#AttributeTok("Mean =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(wellbeing, ");#AttributeTok("na.rm =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("            ");#AttributeTok("SD =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sd");#NormalTok("(wellbeing, ");#AttributeTok("na.rm =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("            ");#AttributeTok(".by =");#NormalTok(" faculty) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("arrange");#NormalTok("(faculty) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  knitr");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("kable");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("digits =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("col.names =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Faculty\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Students\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Mean\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"SD\"");#NormalTok("))");],
[#InformationTok("```");],
[],
[#NormalTok("Wellbeing declined over the programme (@fig-wellbeing), as found in");],
[#NormalTok("earlier studies ");#CommentTok("[");#OtherTok("@author2020");#CommentTok("]");#NormalTok(".");],));
Several new features appear. In the header, #NormalTok("format: docx"); produces a Word document, the format most supervisors want for comments, and #NormalTok("bibliography:"); names a file of references. The setting #NormalTok("execute: echo: false"); hides the code in the finished document, so that a thesis shows only the results; the code is still in the #NormalTok(".qmd"); file for anyone who wants to check it.

In the body, #strong[chunk options], the lines starting with #NormalTok("#|");, control each chunk: #NormalTok("label"); names it, #NormalTok("message: false"); hides package messages, and #NormalTok("tbl-cap"); or #NormalTok("fig-cap"); gives a caption. A chunk labelled #NormalTok("tbl-..."); or #NormalTok("fig-..."); becomes a numbered table or figure, and #NormalTok("@tbl-faculty"); in the text becomes a #strong[cross-reference], "Table 1", with the number filled in automatically. The function #NormalTok("knitr::kable()"); turns a data frame into a formatted table. Finally, #NormalTok("[@author2020]"); is a #strong[citation]: Quarto looks up the key in the bibliography file, formats the citation, and adds the reference list at the end.

When the document is rendered, the first paragraph becomes:

#quote(block: true)[
The sample included 600 graduate students from 5 faculties. Their average wellbeing in the first semester was 60.5 on the wellbeing index (0 to 100). Table 1 shows wellbeing by faculty.
]

and the table chunk produces #ref(<tbl-faculty>, supplement: [Table]):

#figure([
#table(
  columns: 4,
  align: (left,right,right,right,),
  table.header([Faculty], [Students], [Mean], [SD],),
  table.hline(),
  [Education], [148], [61.9], [13.2],
  [Health Sciences], [154], [59.0], [11.6],
  [Humanities], [95], [61.7], [11.7],
  [Natural Sciences], [87], [61.3], [10.5],
  [Social Sciences], [116], [58.9], [12.1],
)
], caption: figure.caption(
position: top, 
[
Wellbeing in the first semester, by faculty.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-tbl", 
supplement: "Table", 
)
<tbl-faculty>


If the data changes, the document is rendered again, and every number, table, and figure is updated. The supervisor's problem from the start of the chapter now takes one click to fix.

=== References and citations
<references-and-citations>
The bibliography file uses the #strong[BibTeX] format, which almost every reference manager (Zotero, Mendeley, EndNote) can export, and Google Scholar can provide for any paper. One entry looks like this:

#Skylighting(([#VariableTok("@book");#NormalTok("{");#OtherTok("kuhn2022");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#DataTypeTok("author");#NormalTok("    = {Kuhn, Max and Silge, Julia},");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#DataTypeTok("title");#NormalTok("     = {Tidy Modeling with R},");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#DataTypeTok("publisher");#NormalTok(" = {O'Reilly Media},");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#DataTypeTok("year");#NormalTok("      = {2022}");],
[#NormalTok("}");],));
The key, #NormalTok("kuhn2022");, is what goes after the #NormalTok("@"); in the text. A #NormalTok("csl:"); line in the YAML header chooses the citation style (APA, Vancouver, Harvard, or any of thousands of journal styles from the Citation Style Language collection), so switching styles never means retyping references. With Zotero, RStudio's visual editor can insert citations directly from your library.

=== Equations
<equations>
Equations are written in #strong[LaTeX] notation between dollar signs: #NormalTok("$\\bar{x} = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum x_i$"); inside a sentence gives $macron(x) = 1 / n sum x_i$, and double dollar signs set an equation on its own line:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("$$");],
[#NormalTok("\\text{logit}(p) = \\beta_0 + \\beta_1 \\times \\text{stress} + \\beta_2 \\times \\text{support}");],
[#NormalTok("$$");],));
$ upright("logit") \( p \) = beta_0 + beta_1 times upright("stress") + beta_2 times upright("support") $

The same notation works in HTML, Word, and PDF output. The equations in this book are all written this way.

=== Output formats
<output-formats>
Changing one line of the YAML header changes the output:

#figure([
#table(
  columns: (33.33%, 33.33%, 33.33%),
  align: (auto,auto,auto,),
  table.header([Format], [YAML], [Notes],),
  table.hline(),
  [Web page], [#NormalTok("format: html");], [Interactive; good for sharing results online],
  [Word], [#NormalTok("format: docx");], [For comments from supervisors; #NormalTok("reference-doc: template.docx"); applies your university's styles],
  [PDF], [#NormalTok("format: pdf");], [Needs a LaTeX installation: run #NormalTok("quarto install tinytex"); once in the Terminal],
  [Slides], [#NormalTok("format: revealjs");], [For presentations],
)
], caption: figure.caption(
position: top, 
[
Common Quarto output formats
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-tbl", 
supplement: "Table", 
)
<tbl-formats>


Several formats can be listed at once, and one render then produces all of them. Many journals and universities provide Quarto templates, installed as #strong[extensions], that format a document to their requirements. A whole thesis can be written as a #strong[Quarto book] (#NormalTok("project: type: book");), with one #NormalTok(".qmd"); file per chapter, exactly like this book.

=== Parameterised reports
<parameterised-reports>
The dean of each faculty would like a one-page summary for their own faculty. Rather than five reports, one report with a #strong[parameter] serves them all:

#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("---");],
[#AnnotationTok("title:");#CommentTok(" \"Wellbeing report\"");],
[#AnnotationTok("format:");#CommentTok(" html");],
[#AnnotationTok("params:");],
[#CommentTok("  faculty: \"Education\"");],
[#CommentTok("---");],
[],
[#InformationTok("```{r}");],
[#NormalTok("faculty_semesters ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" semesters ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("left_join");#NormalTok("(students, ");#FunctionTok("join_by");#NormalTok("(student_id)) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(faculty ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" params");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("faculty)");],
[#InformationTok("```");],
[],
[#NormalTok("This report describes the ");#InformationTok("`r n_distinct(faculty_semesters$student_id)`");#NormalTok(" students");],
[#NormalTok("of the Faculty of ");#InformationTok("`r params$faculty`");#NormalTok(".");],));
The value #NormalTok("params$faculty"); is used in the code like any other value. Rendering from the Terminal with a different value produces each faculty's version:

#Skylighting(([#ExtensionTok("quarto");#NormalTok(" render faculty-report.qmd ");#AttributeTok("-P");#NormalTok(" faculty:");#StringTok("\"Health Sciences\"");],));
== Keeping the same package versions
<keeping-the-same-package-versions>
Packages change. A function's default may change between versions, or a function may be removed, so an analysis that runs today may give different results, or fail, in two years. #strong[renv] records the exact version of every package a project uses, and can restore them later on any computer. It has three main commands, run in the Console:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("renv");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("init");#NormalTok("()      ");#CommentTok("# once: give the project its own package library");],
[#NormalTok("renv");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("snapshot");#NormalTok("()  ");#CommentTok("# after installing or updating packages: record the versions");],
[#NormalTok("renv");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("restore");#NormalTok("()   ");#CommentTok("# on another computer, or later: install the recorded versions");],));
The first command, #NormalTok("renv::init()");, creates a file called #NormalTok("renv.lock");, which lists every package and its version, and gives the project its own private library of packages, so updating a package for one project does not affect the others. Share #NormalTok("renv.lock"); with your code, and anyone can rebuild your exact set of packages.

Even without renv, #strong[report the versions] of R and the key packages in your methods section. R can tell you:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("R.version.string");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] \"R version 4.4.3 (2025-02-28 ucrt)\"");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("packageVersion");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"lme4\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] '1.1.37'");],));
]
]
The function #NormalTok("sessionInfo()"); lists everything that is loaded, for a full record.

== Interactive dashboards with Shiny
<interactive-dashboards-with-shiny>
A report answers the questions its author thought of. Sometimes readers want to ask their own: "what about my faculty?", "what about sleep instead of wellbeing?". #strong[Shiny] turns R code into an interactive web application, without any knowledge of web programming.

A Shiny app rests on three ideas. #strong[Inputs] are the controls the reader uses, such as drop-down menus, buttons, and sliders. #strong[Outputs] are what the app shows in response, such as plots and tables. Every app therefore has two parts: the #strong[user interface] (#NormalTok("ui");), which describes what the user sees, the inputs and the places for the outputs, and the #strong[server] function, which contains the R code that produces the outputs from the inputs. The third idea is what makes Shiny work: #strong[reactivity]. Whenever an input changes, Shiny works out which outputs depend on it and reruns only their code. #ref(<fig-reactivity>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the connections in a wellbeing dashboard: choosing a different faculty updates both the plot and the table, while choosing a different measure updates them without refiltering the data.

