The code follows the three ideas. The layout comes first: the function #NormalTok("page_sidebar()"); from the #strong[bslib] package lays out the page, with a title, a sidebar with the inputs, and the main area with two #strong[cards], one for the plot and one for the table. The inputs are created by #NormalTok("selectInput()");, a drop-down menu whose value is available in the server as #NormalTok("input$faculty");, and by #NormalTok("radioButtons()");, which creates the #NormalTok("input$measure"); choice; the names in #NormalTok("measures"); are shown to the user, and the values are the column names. The outputs are reserved by #NormalTok("plotOutput(\"trend\")"); and #NormalTok("tableOutput(\"summary\")");, and the server fills them by assigning to #NormalTok("output$trend"); and #NormalTok("output$summary"); with #NormalTok("renderPlot()"); and #NormalTok("renderTable()");.

Reactivity is handled by #NormalTok("reactive()");, which creates #NormalTok("selected()");, the data for the chosen faculty. It is recalculated only when #NormalTok("input$faculty"); changes, and both outputs use it. Finally, the expression #NormalTok(".data[[input$measure]]"); picks the column whose name is stored in #NormalTok("input$measure");, the usual way to use a column chosen by the user inside dplyr and ggplot2.

Click #strong[Run App] in RStudio, and the dashboard opens in a window. #ref(<fig-dashboard-plot>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the plot it draws for the Faculty of Education and the wellbeing measure.

