== The reliability of coding
<the-reliability-of-coding>
Two coders who read the same answers will not always choose the same theme. Their raw agreement, the share of answers on which they agree, seems the natural measure of reliability, but it can mislead. When one theme is very common, two coders will often agree simply because both choose it most of the time, even if they are guessing. A small example shows how much this matters. Two coders read 20 answers, 16 of which one coder puts under Workload:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("coder_a ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("rep");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Workload\"");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("16");#NormalTok("), ");#FunctionTok("rep");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Family\"");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("coder_b ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("rep");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Workload\"");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("14");#NormalTok("), ");#FunctionTok("rep");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Family\"");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("),    ");#CommentTok("# the first 16 answers");],
[#NormalTok("             ");#FunctionTok("rep");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Workload\"");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("),  ");#FunctionTok("rep");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Family\"");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("))    ");#CommentTok("# the last 4 answers");],
[],
[#NormalTok("observed ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(coder_a ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" coder_b)");],
[#NormalTok("chance   ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sum");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("prop.table");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("table");#NormalTok("(coder_a)) ");#SpecialCharTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("prop.table");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("table");#NormalTok("(coder_b)))");],
[#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("observed =");#NormalTok(" observed, ");#AttributeTok("chance =");#NormalTok(" chance, ");#AttributeTok("kappa =");#NormalTok(" (observed ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" chance) ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" (");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" chance))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("observed   chance    kappa ");],
[#NormalTok("   0.800    0.680    0.375 ");],));
]
]
The coders agree on 80% of the answers, which sounds good. But each coder puts 80% of the answers under Workload, so two coders who assigned themes at random in those proportions would agree on 68% of them by chance alone. #strong[Cohen's kappa] measures agreement beyond chance: the observed agreement minus the chance agreement, as a share of the most that could be gained over chance @cohen1960. Here it is only 0.37. A kappa of 0 means no better than chance, and 1 means perfect agreement. A common rule of thumb calls 0.41 to 0.60 moderate, 0.61 to 0.80 substantial, and above 0.80 almost perfect agreement @landis1977. The function #NormalTok("kap()"); from the yardstick package gives the same result:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("kap");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("tibble");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("a =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("factor");#NormalTok("(coder_a), ");#AttributeTok("b =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("factor");#NormalTok("(coder_b)), ");#AttributeTok("truth =");#NormalTok(" a, ");#AttributeTok("estimate =");#NormalTok(" b)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 1 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric .estimator .estimate");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>   <chr>          <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1 kap     binary         0.375");],));
]
]
Kappa is the standard measure of #strong[inter-rater reliability] (Chapter 5), and it applies to any pair of coders. Later in this chapter, one of the coders is a keyword list and then a language model, and the other is Elaf.

== How AI assistants work
<how-ai-assistants-work>
The AI assistants that researchers use, such as ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, and Copilot, are built on #strong[large language models] (LLMs). A language model is a neural network (Chapter 15), with billions of weights, trained on an enormous amount of text: books, websites, and code. During training it learns to do one thing extremely well: predict the next word (strictly, the next #strong[token], a word or part of a word) given the text so far. Answering a question means generating a reply one token at a time, each time choosing a likely continuation. Further training on examples of helpful answers turns this into an assistant that follows instructions.

This explains both their strengths and their weaknesses. They are fluent in R, because a huge amount of R code and documentation was in their training text, so they can write, explain, and fix code well, especially for common tasks. But they produce what is #emph[plausible], not what is #emph[verified]: a made-up function name that looks like a real one, a wrong argument, or an outdated way of doing something can be delivered with complete confidence. Such invented content is called a #strong[hallucination]. Their knowledge also stops at the date their training data was collected, so packages that changed since then, as tidymodels and ggplot2 have, may be described as they used to be. And they know nothing about your data unless you tell them, and cannot run your code unless the tool is designed to do so.

The practical rule follows directly: #strong[use AI to draft, and check everything it produces.] The checking is your job, and you remain responsible for the result.

== AI as a coding assistant
<ai-as-a-coding-assistant>
=== Asking good questions
<asking-good-questions>
An assistant can only be as specific as the question. A good request for R code states #strong[the goal] in words, such as "For each faculty, I want the percentage of students who considered dropping out". It describes #strong[the data]: the names and types of the relevant columns, for which the output of #NormalTok("glimpse(students)"); (Chapter 3) is ideal, although real, identifiable data should never be pasted. It names #strong[the tools], such as "Use dplyr with the native pipe #NormalTok("|>");", so that the answer matches a familiar style. And for errors, it gives the exact error message and the code that produced it.

=== Always check the answer
<always-check-the-answer>
Suppose an assistant is asked for the percentage of students in each faculty who considered dropping out, and suggests:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("students ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("summarise");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("percent =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("100");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sum");#NormalTok("(considering_dropout ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("nrow");#NormalTok("(students),");],
[#NormalTok("            ");#AttributeTok(".by =");#NormalTok(" faculty)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("           faculty  percent");],
[#NormalTok("1  Health Sciences 4.833333");],
[#NormalTok("2        Education 2.666667");],
[#NormalTok("3  Social Sciences 3.500000");],
[#NormalTok("4       Humanities 2.166667");],
[#NormalTok("5 Natural Sciences 1.833333");],));
]
]
The code runs without an error and the numbers look reasonable. But they add up to only 15, the percentage of #emph[all] students who considered dropping out: #NormalTok("nrow(students)"); counts every student, not the students in each faculty. The question asked for the share #emph[within] each faculty:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("students ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("summarise");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("percent =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("100");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(considering_dropout ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("            ");#AttributeTok(".by =");#NormalTok(" faculty)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("           faculty  percent");],
[#NormalTok("1  Health Sciences 18.83117");],
[#NormalTok("2        Education 10.81081");],
[#NormalTok("3  Social Sciences 18.10345");],
[#NormalTok("4       Humanities 13.68421");],
[#NormalTok("5 Natural Sciences 12.64368");],));
]
]
The mean of a TRUE/FALSE condition is the proportion of TRUE values (Chapter 6), calculated here within each faculty. The first answer was not a hallucination; it was a plausible misunderstanding, the most dangerous kind of error, because nothing looks wrong. Four checks catch most such errors. Code can be #strong[run on a tiny example] whose answer is known, as every chapter of this book does. A number can be #strong[checked by another route]\; here, #NormalTok("students |> count(faculty, considering_dropout)"); gives the counts to check against. The #strong[help page] of any unfamiliar function (#NormalTok("?summarise");) confirms that it exists and does what the assistant says. And the assistant can be #strong[asked to explain] each line, to see whether the explanation matches what was wanted.

Assistants are also excellent teachers: "explain this code line by line", "why does this give NA?", and "what does this error mean?" are among their most useful questions.

=== AI inside the editor
<ai-inside-the-editor>
Assistants can also work inside RStudio and Positron. #strong[GitHub Copilot], which can be switched on in RStudio's options, suggests code as you type, completing a line or a whole block from a comment such as #NormalTok("# plot wellbeing by semester");. #strong[Positron Assistant] and similar tools add a chat that can see your open files and, with permission, your data's structure. Such suggestions arrive quickly and look authoritative, so the same rule applies: read each suggestion before accepting it. These tools change fast; the ideas in this chapter apply to whichever one you use.

== Coding the open-ended answers
<coding-the-open-ended-answers>
A few of the answers show what the coding must handle:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("open_responses ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("slice");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("5");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("9");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("15");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("55");#NormalTok(")) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("pull");#NormalTok("(biggest_challenge)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] \"most of my income goes on tuition fees. Besides that, I work alone all the time. Things are slowly improving.\"                                                  ");],
[#NormalTok("[2] \"I moved to a city where I know no one for my studies and I feel lonely. And I never have enough time.\"                                                          ");],
[#NormalTok("[3] \"Supervisor.\"                                                                                                                                                    ");],
[#NormalTok("[4] \"The direction of my thesis changed twice after comments from my supervsor. Sometimes I rewrite the same section again and again without knowing if it is right.\"");],));
]
]
They vary in length and style, often mention more than one challenge, and sometimes contain spelling mistakes, as real answers do. The codebook has seven themes, and Elaf coded a random sample of 200 answers into the theme that each student presents as their main challenge:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("open_responses_coded ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("count");#NormalTok("(theme, ");#AttributeTok("sort =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("        theme  n");],
[#NormalTok("1 Supervision 68");],
[#NormalTok("2    Workload 45");],
[#NormalTok("3   Isolation 31");],
[#NormalTok("4      Health 20");],
[#NormalTok("5      Family 14");],
[#NormalTok("6    Finances 14");],
[#NormalTok("7       Other  8");],));
]
]
The hand-coded sample is the benchmark for any automatic method, exactly like the test set of Chapter 11.

=== Coding by keywords
<coding-by-keywords>
The simplest automatic method is a #strong[dictionary]: a list of keywords for each theme. Each answer is assigned to the theme whose keywords it contains most often, and to "Other" if it contains none. The function #NormalTok("str_count()"); from stringr counts the matches of a #strong[regular expression], a text pattern in which #NormalTok("|"); means "or" and #NormalTok("\\\\b"); marks the start of a word:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("keywords ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("Supervision =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"");#SpecialCharTok("\\\\");#StringTok("b(supervis|feedback|guidance|meeting)\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("Workload    =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"");#SpecialCharTok("\\\\");#StringTok("b(time|deadline|workload|too much|assignment|reading|busy)\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("Finances    =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"");#SpecialCharTok("\\\\");#StringTok("b(money|fee|scholarship|rent|afford|salary|income|funding|stipend|loan)\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("Family      =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"");#SpecialCharTok("\\\\");#StringTok("b(famil|child|kid|son");#SpecialCharTok("\\\\");#StringTok("b|daughter|baby|parent|mother|father|husband|wife)\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("Health      =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"");#SpecialCharTok("\\\\");#StringTok("b(sleep|tired|stress|anxi|health|ill");#SpecialCharTok("\\\\");#StringTok("b|burnout|headache|coffee|exhaust)\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("Isolation   =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"");#SpecialCharTok("\\\\");#StringTok("b(lonel|alone|friends|outsider|miss (home|my)|far from|know no one)\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("Other       =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"");#SpecialCharTok("\\\\");#StringTok("b(ethic|participant|statistic|library|procedure|english|power cut|laptop|internet|software)\"");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("code_by_keywords ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#ControlFlowTok("function");#NormalTok("(answer) {");],
[#NormalTok("  hits ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sapply");#NormalTok("(keywords, \\(pattern) ");#FunctionTok("str_count");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("str_to_lower");#NormalTok("(answer), pattern))");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#ControlFlowTok("if");#NormalTok(" (");#FunctionTok("all");#NormalTok("(hits ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(")) ");#StringTok("\"Other\"");#NormalTok(" ");#ControlFlowTok("else");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("names");#NormalTok("(keywords)[");#FunctionTok("which.max");#NormalTok("(hits)]");],
[#NormalTok("}");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("code_by_keywords");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"I miss my family and I cannot afford the fees.\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] \"Finances\"");],));
]
]
The example answer matches one Family keyword ("family"), one Isolation phrase ("miss my"), and two Finances keywords ("afford" and "fees"), so it is coded as Finances, although a human reader might well code it differently. Applying the function to the hand-coded answers:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("themes ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("names");#NormalTok("(keywords)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("keyword_check ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" open_responses_coded ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("keyword_theme =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sapply");#NormalTok("(biggest_challenge, code_by_keywords),");],
[#NormalTok("         ");#AttributeTok("theme =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("factor");#NormalTok("(theme, ");#AttributeTok("levels =");#NormalTok(" themes),");],
[#NormalTok("         ");#AttributeTok("keyword_theme =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("factor");#NormalTok("(keyword_theme, ");#AttributeTok("levels =");#NormalTok(" themes))");],
[],
[#NormalTok("keyword_check ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("accuracy");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("truth =");#NormalTok(" theme, ");#AttributeTok("estimate =");#NormalTok(" keyword_theme)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 1 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric  .estimator .estimate");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>    <chr>          <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1 accuracy multiclass      0.61");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("keyword_check ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("kap");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("truth =");#NormalTok(" theme, ");#AttributeTok("estimate =");#NormalTok(" keyword_theme)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 1 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric .estimator .estimate");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>   <chr>          <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1 kap     multiclass     0.512");],));
]
]
The keywords agree with the hand coding for 61% of the answers, and their Cohen's kappa, the agreement beyond chance explained at the start of the chapter, is 0.51: moderate agreement. They fail because meaning is not in single words: "my supervisor" in "I feel I am letting down both my family and my supervisor" is about family, and "I never have enough time" can be about workload or about children. Reading for meaning is exactly what language models are good at.

=== Calling a language model from R
<calling-a-language-model-from-r>
The #strong[ellmer] package connects R to language models of two kinds.

#strong[Online models] from providers: #NormalTok("chat_anthropic()"); for Claude, #NormalTok("chat_openai()"); for ChatGPT's models, and #NormalTok("chat_google_gemini()"); for Gemini. They are the most capable, but using them from code requires an #strong[API key], a secret password linked to your account, and each request costs a small amount. Store the key in your personal #NormalTok(".Renviron"); file (#NormalTok("usethis::edit_r_environ()"); opens it), never in a script that others may see:

#Skylighting(([#VariableTok("ANTHROPIC_API_KEY");#OperatorTok("=");#NormalTok("your-key-here");],));
#strong[Local models], which run on your own computer through #strong[Ollama] (#link("https://ollama.com")[ollama.com]), a free program that downloads and runs open models. They need no key and cost nothing, and the text never leaves your computer. Install Ollama, download a model once in the Terminal (for example #NormalTok("ollama pull gemma4:e4b");, a model of about 10 GB), and use #NormalTok("chat_ollama()");. A computer with a graphics card (GPU) makes them much faster.

Either way, a chat then works much like a chat website, from R:

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(ellmer)");],
[#NormalTok("chat ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("chat_ollama");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("model =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"gemma4:e4b\"");#NormalTok(")              ");#CommentTok("# local");],
[#CommentTok("# chat <- chat_anthropic(model = \"claude-sonnet-5\")   # online, needs a key");],
[#NormalTok("chat");#SpecialCharTok("$");#FunctionTok("chat");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"In one sentence, what does Cohen's kappa measure?\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
The #NormalTok("model"); argument names the exact model. Always set it explicitly and record it: models are updated and retired, and different models give different answers.

For the study, a local model was chosen, Google's Gemma 4 (the #NormalTok("e4b"); version), for two reasons: the students' answers never leave the computer, and anyone can repeat the analysis for free.

=== Structured answers for many texts
<structured-answers-for-many-texts>
Coding needs two things that a chat on a website does not give: the same instructions for every answer, and a reply in a fixed format that R can use. The #strong[system prompt] holds the instructions: the task and the codebook, with a definition of each theme. With an online model, a #strong[type] describes the required answer: here, an object with one field, #NormalTok("theme");, which must be one of the seven themes:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("codebook ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"");],
[#StringTok("You are helping a researcher code open-ended survey answers from graduate");],
[#StringTok("students, who were asked: 'What has been your biggest challenge during your");],
[#StringTok("studies?' Assign each answer to exactly ONE theme: the main challenge the");],
[#StringTok("student describes. If several challenges are mentioned, choose the one the");],
[#StringTok("student presents first or as most important.");],
[],
[#StringTok("Themes:");],
[#StringTok("- Supervision: the supervisor; feedback, guidance, meetings, disagreements.");],
[#StringTok("- Workload: too much work or too little time; deadlines, coursework, reading.");],
[#StringTok("- Finances: money, fees, scholarships, living costs, paid work to pay for study.");],
[#StringTok("- Family: children, partners, parents, caring duties, studying at home.");],
[#StringTok("- Health: physical or mental health, sleep, stress, anxiety, burnout, illness.");],
[#StringTok("- Isolation: loneliness, being far from home, no colleagues or friends.");],
[#StringTok("- Other: anything else, such as ethics approval, statistics, academic English.");],
[#StringTok("\"");],
[],
[#NormalTok("theme_type ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("type_object");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("theme =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("type_enum");#NormalTok("(themes, ");#StringTok("\"The single main theme of the answer.\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("chat ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("chat_anthropic");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("system_prompt =");#NormalTok(" codebook, ");#AttributeTok("model =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"claude-sonnet-5\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("                       ");#AttributeTok("params =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("params");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("temperature =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok("))");],
[],
[#NormalTok("ai_codes ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("parallel_chat_structured");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("  chat,");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("prompts =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("as.list");#NormalTok("(open_responses");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("biggest_challenge),");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("type =");#NormalTok(" theme_type");],
[#NormalTok(")");],));
The function #NormalTok("type_enum()"); restricts the answer to the listed themes, so the model cannot invent a new one or reply with a paragraph. The setting #NormalTok("temperature = 0"); asks the model to choose its most likely answer every time, rather than sampling more freely, which makes the results as repeatable as possible. #NormalTok("parallel_chat_structured()"); sends one request per answer, several at a time, and returns a data frame with one row per answer and a #NormalTok("theme"); column.

Smaller local models do not always follow such a format. When #NormalTok("parallel_chat_structured()"); was tried with the local model, it answered correctly ("Finances", "Workload") but as plain words, not in the structured format, and ellmer could not read the replies. The solution is to add one line to the end of the codebook, "Reply with the name of the theme only", to ask for plain text with #NormalTok("parallel_chat_text()");, and to let R check each reply against the list of themes:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("chat ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("chat_ollama");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("system_prompt =");#NormalTok(" codebook, ");#AttributeTok("model =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"gemma4:e4b\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("                    ");#AttributeTok("params =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("params");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("temperature =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok("))");],
[],
[#NormalTok("replies ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("parallel_chat_text");#NormalTok("(chat, ");#FunctionTok("as.list");#NormalTok("(open_responses");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("biggest_challenge))");],
[#NormalTok("ai_codes ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" themes[");#FunctionTok("match");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("tolower");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("gsub");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"[^A-Za-z]\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"\"");#NormalTok(", replies)), ");#FunctionTok("tolower");#NormalTok("(themes))]");],));
The function #NormalTok("gsub()"); removes anything that is not a letter, such as a full stop, and #NormalTok("match()"); finds the reply in the list of themes, ignoring capital letters. A reply that is not one of the themes becomes #NormalTok("NA");, so it is counted rather than silently accepted. Checking a model's output in code like this is good practice with any model.

The full script, #NormalTok("data-raw/run_ai_coding.R"); in the book's repository, also records the provider, the model, the date, the software versions, and the time taken, and codes the 200 hand-coded answers a second time to check that the model gives the same answers when asked again. It was run once, and the results were saved to a file, so that this chapter can analyse them without calling the model again.

=== Agreement with the hand coding
<agreement-with-the-hand-coding>
The script used the model gemma4:e4b, run locally through Ollama, on 24 September 2026. The 735 requests took about 3 minutes, at no cost, and every reply was one of the seven themes. The results are joined to the hand-coded answers:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("ai ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("read.csv");#NormalTok("(ai_file)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("ai_check ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" open_responses_coded ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("left_join");#NormalTok("(ai, ");#FunctionTok("join_by");#NormalTok("(student_id)) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("across");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(theme, ai_theme, ai_theme_rerun), \\(x) ");#FunctionTok("factor");#NormalTok("(x, ");#AttributeTok("levels =");#NormalTok(" themes)))");],
[],
[#NormalTok("ai_check ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("accuracy");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("truth =");#NormalTok(" theme, ");#AttributeTok("estimate =");#NormalTok(" ai_theme)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 1 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  .metric  .estimator .estimate");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>    <chr>          <dbl>");],
