<fig-project>


The path is not a straight line in practice. Exploring the data sends you back to cleaning when you find a problem; a model's diagnostics send you back to exploring. What matters is that each step is written in code, so that going back and rerunning everything is easy.

=== Organising the project
<organising-the-project>
A project that others (and your future self) can follow has a predictable structure. The final thesis project looks like this:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("wellbeing-thesis/");],
[#NormalTok("├── wellbeing-thesis.Rproj      the RStudio Project (Chapter 1)");],
[#NormalTok("├── README.txt                  what the project is and how to run it");],
[#NormalTok("├── data-raw/");],
[#NormalTok("│   └── wellbeing_raw.xlsx      the survey export, never edited by hand");],
[#NormalTok("├── data/                       clean data, created by the scripts");],
[#NormalTok("├── R/");],
[#NormalTok("│   ├── 01-clean-data.R         raw export -> clean tables (Chapter 3)");],
[#NormalTok("│   └── 02-analysis.R           models and figures");],
[#NormalTok("├── output/                     figures and tables, created by the scripts");],
[#NormalTok("├── results.qmd                 the results chapter (Chapter 17)");],
[#NormalTok("├── references.bib");],
[#NormalTok("└── renv.lock                   package versions (Chapter 17)");],));
Four principles lie behind it. #strong[Raw data is read-only]: everything in #NormalTok("data/"); and #NormalTok("output/"); can be deleted and recreated by running the scripts. #strong[Scripts are numbered] in the order they run, and each does one job. #strong[Paths are relative to the project], using #NormalTok("here::here()"); (Chapter 1), so the project runs on any computer. And #strong[the README] says in a few lines what the project is and how to run it.

The downloadable project for this chapter has the same structure, with working scripts (its #NormalTok("data/"); and #NormalTok("output/"); folders are created when the scripts run, and it has no #NormalTok("renv.lock");, which you create with #NormalTok("renv::init()"); for your own project). The rest of the chapter walks through what the scripts do.

