[#NormalTok("  ");#StringTok("\"ellmer\"");],
[#NormalTok("))");],));
The #strong[tidyverse] package installs dplyr, tidyr, stringr, readr, ggplot2, and several others in one go. The first time you install packages, R may ask you to choose a #strong[CRAN mirror] (choose #emph[0-Cloud], which is fast everywhere) and whether to use a personal library (answer #emph[Yes]).

=== The student wellbeing data: the data2thesis package
<the-student-wellbeing-data-the-data2thesis-package>
Elaf's data is in the #strong[data2thesis] package, which is installed from this book's website rather than from CRAN:

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("install.packages");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"https://polla-fattah.github.io/data2thesis_r/downloads/data2thesis_1.1.0.tar.gz\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("                 ");#AttributeTok("repos =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("NULL");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("type =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"source\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
The package contains only data, so it installs on every system without compiling anything. Check that it works:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(data2thesis)");],
[#FunctionTok("nrow");#NormalTok("(students)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 600");],));
]
]
The same data is also available as ordinary files (CSV, Excel, and SPSS) on the book's website, for readers who prefer to import files, as Chapter 2 does.

